          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;On the Calendar 🗓&lt;/b&gt;" />
          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Recently Made 🍽&lt;/b&gt;">
            <outline text="Zucchini and Tomato Risotto">
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/zucchini-sun-dried-tomato-risotto-63d94c46537e2ba2250b2159&quot;&gt;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/zucchini-sun-dried-tomato-risotto-63d94c46537e2ba2250b2159&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Magnifico Mozz Melts" _note="with Balsamic Shallot &amp; Bell Pepper Relish, Basil Aioli &amp; Crispy Potatoes">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="1 Bell Pepper" />
                <outline text="1 Shallot" />
                <outline text="Balsamic Vinegar" _note="5 tsp " />
                <outline text="Mayonnaise" _note="4 TBSP " />
                <outline text="Mozzarella cheese (Shredded)" _note="1 Cup " />
                <outline text="Yukon Gold Potatoes" _note="12 oz " />
                <outline text="Garlic Powder" _note="11 tsp " />
                <outline text="Basil" _note="1/2 oz " />
                <outline text="Sourdough Bread" _note="4 Slices " />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Prep">
                  <outline text="Adjust rack to top position and preheat oven to 425 degrees. " />
                  <outline text="Wash and dry all produce. " />
                  <outline text="Halve bell pepper lengthwise; remove stem and seeds. " />
                  <outline text="Cut potatoes into ½-inch-thick wedges. " />
                  <outline text="Halve, peel, and slice shallot." />
                  <outline text="Toss shallot on a piece of foil with a drizzle of olive oil, half the vinegar, salt, and pepper; cinch into a packet." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Roast Veggies">
                  <outline text="Toss potatoes on one side of a baking sheet with a drizzle of olive oil, half the garlic powder (you’ll use more later), salt, and pepper." />
                  <outline text="Place bell pepper and foil packet with shallot on opposite side of same sheet. " />
                  <outline text="Drizzle bell pepper with olive oil and season with salt and pepper; rub to coat. " />
                  <outline text="Arrange cut sides down." _note="For 4 servings, toss potatoes on one sheet; place bell peppers and foil packet with shallots on a second sheet." />
                  <outline text="Roast on top rack until bell pepper and shallot are browned and tender, 15-20 minutes (you’ll roast potatoes a bit longer)." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Finish Potatoes">
                  <outline text="Once bell pepper and shallot are done, transfer to a cutting board. " />
                  <outline text="Give potatoes a toss. " />
                  <outline text="Return to top rack until browned and crispy, 5-10 minutes more. " />
                  <outline text="Turn off oven; leave potatoes inside to keep warm until ready to serve." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Make Aioli">
                  <outline text="Meanwhile, pick basil leaves from stems; mince leaves." />
                  <outline text="In a small bowl, combine mayonnaise, minced basil, and a big pinch of remaining garlic powder to taste. " />
                  <outline text="Season with salt and pepper." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Assemble Melts">
                  <outline text="Finely chop bell pepper and balsamic shallot together to form a relish. " />
                  <outline text="Season with salt and pepper." />
                  <outline text="Spread half the sourdough slices with relish; sprinkle with mozzarella. " />
                  <outline text="Spread remaining slices with half the basil aioli. " />
                  <outline text="Top mozzarella with remaining slices, aioli-sides down." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Finish &amp; Serve">
                  <outline text="Heat a large drizzle of olive oil in a large pan over medium heat. " />
                  <outline text="Add melts and cook until bread is golden brown and cheese has melted, 4-6 minutes per side." />
                  <outline text="&lt;i&gt;TIP: If necessary, add another drizzle of olive oil after flipping sandwiches.&lt;/i&gt;" />
                  <outline text="Halve melts on a diagonal. " />
                  <outline text="Divide between plates and serve with potatoes and remaining aioli on the side." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="HelloFresh: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/magnifico-mozz-melts-5f3aeef9ad6a05609c7d8cae&quot;&gt;Web&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href=&quot;https://hellofresh.com/recipecards/card/magnifico-mozz-melts-5f3aeef9ad6a05609c7d8cae-0649420a.pdf&quot;&gt;PDF&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://app.speechify.com/item/8c75713b-114f-4d4c-a047-bae46922f68f&quot;&gt;Voice Recipe&lt;/a&gt;">
                <outline text="Alright, let’s get this going. First things first, go ahead and get your oven ready. You’ll want to move the rack up to the very top position and preheat it to 425 degrees Fahrenheit. We’re going to be roasting some veggies, so that heat is important." />
                <outline text="Now for some prep work. You’ve got a bell pepper, some potatoes, and a shallot. Let’s get all of that washed and dried. Once that’s done, grab your bell pepper and cut it in half lengthwise. Go ahead and take out the stem and all the seeds." />
                <outline text="Next, the potatoes. Cut those into half-inch-thick wedges. No need to be exact, just get them all roughly the same size so they cook evenly. Then, peel and slice your shallot." />
                <outline text="We're going to make a little foil packet for the shallot. Rip off a piece of foil, put the sliced shallot in the center, and drizzle a little olive oil over it. Add about half of your vinegar—just a splash. Season it up with some salt and pepper, then cinch up the foil to make a nice little packet." />
                <outline text="Time to get things into the oven. Grab a baking sheet. Toss your potatoes on one side with a drizzle of olive oil. Add half the garlic powder, plus some salt and pepper. Give it a good toss to make sure all the potatoes are coated." />
                <outline text="Now, on the other side of that same baking sheet, place your bell pepper and the shallot foil packet. Drizzle the bell pepper with a little more olive oil and season it with salt and pepper. Rub it all in to make sure it’s coated, and make sure to lay it down with the cut side facing the sheet." />
                <outline text="Okay, pop that sheet into the oven on the top rack. We're going to roast the bell pepper and shallot until they're nice and tender, about 15 to 20 minutes. Don’t worry about the potatoes yet; they'll need a bit more time." />
                <outline text="Once the bell pepper and shallot are done, pull them out and transfer them to a cutting board. Give the potatoes a quick toss on the sheet pan, then put the pan back in the oven. The potatoes need another 5 to 10 minutes to get golden brown and crispy. After that, go ahead and turn the oven off, but leave the potatoes in there so they stay warm until we're ready to eat." />
                <outline text="While that's happening, let's make the aioli. Grab your basil and pick the leaves off the stems, then mince them up finely. In a small bowl, mix the mayonnaise, your minced basil, and a big pinch of the remaining garlic powder. Season it with salt and pepper to taste. You can always add more if you like." />
                <outline text="Next up, the relish! Grab that roasted bell pepper and shallot you set aside. We're going to chop them up finely together. Mix them until they form a nice relish, then season it with salt and pepper." />
                <outline text="Now for the melts themselves. Spread the relish on half of your sourdough slices, and sprinkle a nice layer of mozzarella over the top. On the other sourdough slices, spread the basil aioli you just made. Put the aioli-covered slices on top of the mozzarella, with the aioli side facing down." />
                <outline text="Almost there! Get a large pan and heat up a generous drizzle of olive oil over medium heat. When the pan is hot, add your sandwiches and cook them until the bread is golden brown and the cheese is beautifully melted. This should take about 4 to 6 minutes per side. If the pan looks a little dry after you flip them, don’t hesitate to add a little more olive oil." />
                <outline text="When they’re done, take them out of the pan and cut each melt in half on a diagonal. Plate them up, add the potatoes, and put the rest of that delicious aioli on the side for dipping. And that’s it, you're all set! Enjoy." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#vegetarian " />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Sloppy Joes">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="ground beef #ALDI " _note="2 ½ lbs" />
                <outline text="1 green pepper #Sprouts " />
                <outline text="1/2 an onion #Sprouts " />
                <outline text="5 garlic cloves " />
                <outline text="ketchup" _note="1½ cups" />
                <outline text="brown sugar" _note="2 tbsp" />
                <outline text="chili powder" _note="2 tsp" />
                <outline text="mustard" _note="1 tsp" />
                <outline text="red pepper flakes" _note="1/2 tsp" />
                <outline text="worcestershire sauce (to taste)" />
                <outline text="tabasco sauce (to taste)" />
                <outline text="salt &amp; pepper" />
                <outline text="8 kaiser rolls or any kind of buns #ALDI " _note="Hawaiian sweet rolls" />
                <outline text="butter" _note="2 tbsp" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Brown beef, drain fat" />
                <outline text="Add green pepper and onion, stir" />
                <outline text="Add garlic and ketchup" />
                <outline text="Add 1 cup of water and stir" />
                <outline text="Add brown sugar, spices, and sauces" />
                <outline text="Cover and heat for 20 minutes" />
                <outline text="Soften rolls with butter" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Pioneer Woman" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#8_servings #kyles_favorite #sandwich #lasts_multiple_days " />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Ari’s Potatoe Soup">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="1 stick of butter" />
                <outline text="Garlic salt, to taste" />
                <outline text="Poultry seasoning, to taste" />
                <outline text="White pepper, to taste" />
                <outline text="2 to 3 large tablespoons chicken base" />
                <outline text="Flour, amount for a roux" />
                <outline text="Leftover mashed potatoes, amount for a thick soup" />
                <outline text="4 cups milk" />
                <outline text="3 cups heavy cream" />
                <outline text="1 whole bag of shredded cheddar cheese" />
                <outline text="Scallions for garnish" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Instructions">
                <outline text="In a pot, melt the butter over medium heat." />
                <outline text="Add garlic salt, poultry seasoning, and white pepper to the melted butter. Let the spices toast for a moment." />
                <outline text="Stir in 2-3 tablespoons of chicken base and whisk until dissolved." />
                <outline text="Whisk in an equal amount of flour to the butter and make a roux, cooking for 1 minute." />
                <outline text="Add the leftover mashed potatoes to the pot and smash them down with a spoon." />
                <outline text="Immediately pour in the milk and heavy cream, whisking continuously to prevent the butter from burning and to break up the mashed potatoes." />
                <outline text="Let the soup come to a simmer, stirring occasionally." />
                <outline text="Add more chicken base and poultry seasoning to taste." />
                <outline text="Remove the pot from the heat and stir in the entire bag of cheddar cheese until melted and smooth." />
                <outline text="Serve warm, garnished with scallions." />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text=" Provençal Herbed Chicken " _note="with Burst Tomatoes and Cheesy Pesto Orzo">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="Chicken Breasts" _note="12 oz" />
                <outline text="Herbes de Provence" _note="1 TSPB" />
                <outline text="1 Shallot" />
                <outline text="Grape Tomatoes" _note="4 oz" />
                <outline text="Garlic" _note="2 cloves" />
                <outline text="Parsley" _note="1/4 oz" />
                <outline text="Orzo Pasta" _note="6 oz" />
                <outline text="Red Wine Vinegar" _note="5 tsp" />
                <outline text="1 Chicken Demi-Glace #needsattention " />
                <outline text="Pesto" _note="2 oz" />
                <outline text="Parmesan Cheese" _note="1/2 cup&#10;" />
                <outline text="(Optional, Sprouts French Baget)" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Cook Chicken&lt;/b&gt;: Bring a medium pot of salted water to a boil. Pat chicken dry with paper towels; season all over with salt, pepper, and 1 tsp Herbes de Provence (2 tsp for 4 servings).Heat a drizzle of olive oil in a large pan over medium-high heat. Add chicken and cook until browned and cooked through, 5-7 minutes per side. Remove chicken from pan and set aside. Wipe out pan." />
                <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Prep&lt;/b&gt;: Meanwhile, wash and dry all produce. Halve, peel, and thinly slice shallot. Halve tomatoes. Mince or grate garlic. Pick parsley leaves from stems; discard stems. Finely chop leaves." />
                <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Cook Orzo&lt;/b&gt;: Once water boils, add half the orzo to pot (use all the orzo for 4 servings). Cook, stirring occasionally, until al dente, 9-11 minutes. Drain thoroughly and return to pot." />
                <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Make Pan Sauce&lt;/b&gt;: Heat a drizzle of olive oil in same pan used to cook chicken over medium heat. Add shallot; cook until softened, 2-3 minutes. Increase heat to medium-high and add tomatoes, garlic, ½ tsp sugar (1 tsp for 4 servings), ½ tsp remaining Herbes de Provence (1 tsp for 4), and half the vinegar (all for 4). Cook until thickened and saucy, 2 minutes. Stir in demi-glace and 2 TBSP water. Bring to a simmer, then turn off heat. Stir in 1 TBSP butter (2 TBSP for 4). Season with salt and pepper." />
                <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Finish Orzo&lt;/b&gt;: Stir pesto, 1 TBSP butter, and half the Parmesan into pot with drained orzo. Season with salt and pepper." />
                <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Finish and Serve&lt;/b&gt;: Divide chicken and orzo between plates. Top chicken with pan sauce,making sure to include tomatoes. (TIP: Serve the sauce on the side for picky eaters.) Garnish with parsley and remaining Parmesan " />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#kyles_favorite " />
              <outline text="Source: HelloFresh &lt;a href=&quot;https://ddw4dkk7s1lkt.cloudfront.net/card/provencal-herbed-chicken-5ca4f6b9c445fa4969630c52-e052d82b.pdf&quot;&gt;PDF&lt;/a&gt;" />
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Chef’s Kept Secret Chickpea Caesar " _note="with Grape Tomatoes &amp; Garlicky Croutons&#10;">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="13.4 ounce Chickpeas" />
                <outline text="1 tablespoon Ranch Spice" />
                <outline text="1 Lemon (lemon juice is fine)" />
                <outline text="6 tablespoon Mayonnaise" />
                <outline text="2 tablespoon Soy Sauce" />
                <outline text="2 teaspoon Garlic Powder" />
                <outline text="2 teaspoon Dijon Mustard" />
                <outline text="1 teaspoon Hot Sauce" />
                <outline text="1 unit Demi Baguette" />
                <outline text="¼ cup Parmesan Cheese" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions ">
                <outline text="1. Roast Chickpeas">
                  <outline text="Adjust rack to top position and preheat oven to 425 degrees." _note="(top and middle positions for 4 servings)&#10;" />
                  <outline text="Drain and rinse &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;chickpeas&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;; dry thoroughly with paper towels. " />
                  <outline text="In a small bowl, toss a large drizzle of &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;oil&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, half the &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;Ranch Spice&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, and ½ tsp &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;salt&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. (Prep in a bowl before tossing on baking sheet)" _note="(use all the Ranch spice and 1 tsp of salt for 4 servings.)&#10;" />
                  <outline text="Roast on top rack until chickpeas are lightly crispy, 18-20 minutes" _note="It’s natural for chickpeas to pop a bit while roasting.&#10;">
                    <outline text="(you’ll add more to the sheet after 13 minutes). " _note="So set a 13 min timer&#10;" />
                  </outline>
                </outline>
                <outline text="2. Prep &amp; Make Dressing">
                  <outline text="Meanwhile, wash and dry produce." />
                  <outline text="Quarter &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lemon&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Chop &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lettuce&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; into bite-size pieces until you have 4 cups (8 cups for 4 servings). " />
                  <outline text="Halve &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;tomatoes&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; lengthwise." />
                  <outline text="In a large bowl, whisk together &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;mayonnaise&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, half the &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;mustard&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, half the &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;hot sauce&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, 1 tsp &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;soy sauce&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; (we sent more), ½ tsp &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;garlic powder&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, and juice from two &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lemon wedges&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;." _note="TIP: If dressing seems too thick, add water ½ tsp at a time.&#10;">
                    <outline text="&lt;i&gt;(For 4, use all the mustard, all the hot sauce, 2 tsp soy sauce, 1 tsp garlic powder, and juice from whole lemon.)&lt;/i&gt;" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Season generously with &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;pepper&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="3. Make Croutons">
                  <outline text="Cut &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;baguette&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; into ½-inch cubes." _note="TIP: Slice baguette lengthwise, like you’re making a sandwich, then cut each half into ½-inch strips to make cubing extra-easy.&#10;" />
                  <outline text="In a medium bowl, toss bread with remaining &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;garlic powder&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, 2 TBSP &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;olive oil&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;salt&lt;/mark&gt;, and &lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;pepper&lt;/mark&gt; until evenly coated." _note="(4 TBSP of olive oil for 4 servings) &#10;" />
                  <outline text="Once chickpeas have roasted 13 minutes, remove sheet from oven. Carefully push chickpeas to one side of sheet. Spread bread out on empty side." _note="For 4 servings, leave chickpeas roasting. Use a second sheet for bread; bake on middle rack.&#10;" />
                  <outline text="Return sheet to top rack until croutons are golden brown and crisp, 5-7 minutes." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="4. Assemble Salad &amp; Serve">
                  <outline text="Add lettuce to bowl with dressing; toss to thoroughly coat." _note="&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;Make sure &lt;b&gt;no&lt;/b&gt; dressing is left on the &lt;b&gt;bottom&lt;/b&gt; of the bowl!&lt;/mark&gt;&#10;" />
                  <outline text="Add tomatoes, croutons, &lt;i&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;half&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt; the chickpeas&lt;/mark&gt;, and &lt;i&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;half&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt; the Parmesan&lt;/mark&gt;; toss to combine." />
                  <outline text="Divide salad between plates. " />
                  <outline text="Top with remaining &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;chickpeas&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; and remaining &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;Parmesan&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Serve with any remaining &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lemon wedges&lt;/mark&gt; &lt;/b&gt;on the side." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/chefs-kept-secret-chickpea-caesar-61670d8b3d3e3872c5707a6f&quot;&gt;Webpage&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href=&quot;https://hellofresh.com/recipecards/card/chefs-kept-secret-chickpea-caesar-61670d8b3d3e3872c5707a6f-501dad42.pdf?_gl=1*1ycfpey*_ga*MTcyMjMwNzYzNy4xNjY2NzI5NDA4*_ga_6XTFRPBHKQ*MTY2NjcyOTQwNy4xLjEuMTY2NjcyOTU3Mi42MC4wLjA.&quot;&gt;PDF&lt;/a&gt;" />
              <outline text="#easy" />
              <outline text="#light " />
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://app.speechify.com/item/a738046e-84d5-4799-a079-de2542d1e4b3&quot;&gt;Voice Recipe&lt;/a&gt; 🎧 ">
                <outline text="Okay, let's get cooking! Today we're making Chef’s Kept Secret Chickpea Caesar." />
                <outline text="First, let's gather your ingredients. Let's start with the pantry. Head over to the pantry and grab a can of chickpeas or garbanzo beans (as they may be called). And for seasoning, grab both the garlic powder and Hidden Valley Ranch seasoning. From the fridge, you'll need grape tomatoes. A baby head of lettuce. Mayonnaise. Soy sauce. Dijon mustard. maybe some hot sauce. Parmesan cheese. and a lemon. From the counter, grab the demi baguette. The lemon may also be on the counter. For and cookware, Pull out a strainer. Three large bowls. And maybe the can opener (depending on the top of the chickpea can). From the utensil drawer, grab a knife and a spoon. And probably the huge serrated bread knife." />
                <outline text="Now, let's start prepping. Walk over to the oven and preheat the oven to 425 degrees. Next grab the can of chickpeas and the strainer. Open the can of chickpeas. Then, drain and rinse the chickpeas. Grab a couple paper towels and try to dry them thoroughly with the paper towels. Grab a large bowl and toss the chickpeas with a drizzle of olive oil, half of the Ranch Spice, and a little salt." />
                <outline text="After the chickpeas are nice and coated, toss them onto a baking sheet and put on the top rack to roast on until the chickpeas are lightly crispy. You'll be adding the croutons to the sheet after 13 minutes, so set a timer for 13 minutes called &quot;Pull out chickpeas&quot;." />
                <outline text="While the chickpeas roast, let's prepare the salad and dressing. Wash and dry the grape tomatoes and baby lettuce. Cut the lettuce into bite-sized pieces, remembering to chop them into smaller pieces for a more tender texture. For the amount of lettuce, the recipe calls for 4 cups. So try to visual each handful into a huge measuring cup. Or grab that huge 2 cup measuring cup, if you want to go nuts with it. Otherwise, let's halve the tomatoes lengthwise. " />
                <outline text="To prep the dressing, grab another large bowl and a teaspoon and a regular spoon. Add 6 tablespoons of mayonnaise to the bowl (with the regular spoon) along with one teaspoon of the Dijon mustard, 1 teaspoon of soy sauce, two teaspoons of garlic powder, and the juice from two lemon wedges. Whisk all of that together in the bowl. Season generously with pepper. For a tangier flavor, consider adding a splash of red wine vinegar. (The red wine vinegar part wasn't in the original recipe, but what the heck. Thanks for the tip Gemini.) Great. Set that aside." />
                <outline text="Next, let's make some croutons. Grab another large bowl and a bread knife to cut the baguette into half-inch cubes. Once they're all cut up, toss them in a medium bowl with the a teaspoon of garlic powder, some olive oil, a bit of salt, and good ole pepper, and a small handful of the breadcrumbs. (Again, Gemini is sprinkling in the breadcrumb bit. It claims: A small handful of breadcrumbs will help to bind the croutons and give them a more crispy texture.)  Anyways, spread the croutons out on a baking sheet, and place them in the oven to roast alongside the chickpeas." />
                <outline text="Hopefully, you've done this all in under 13 minutes and you're timer to add the croutons is about to go off. Once chickpeas have roasted 13 minutes, remove sheet from oven. Carefully push chickpeas to one side of sheet and spread the croutons out on empty side. Return the sheet to the oven and continue roasting for another 5-7 minutes, or until the croutons are golden brown. So set a new timer for 5 minutes." />
                <outline text="While the chickpeas and croutons roast, let's assemble the salad. In the large bowl of dressing, add in the lettuce. Toss to coat thoroughly. Make sure &lt;b&gt;no&lt;/b&gt; dressing is left on the &lt;b&gt;bottom&lt;/b&gt; of the bowl! Toss it well. Next add in the halved grape tomatoes, croutons, half of the roasted chickpeas, and some Parmesan cheese." />
                <outline text="Grab a couple plates and divide the salad between the plates. " />
                <outline text="Top each salad with the remaining chickpeas and sprinkle the salad with grated Parmesan cheese. This will add a salty, nutty flavor that complements the other ingredients." />
                <outline text="If you'd like, add a little hot sauce to the bowl designated for Kyle." />
                <outline text="Serve with any remaining &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lemon wedges&lt;/mark&gt; &lt;/b&gt;on the side." />
                <outline text="Serve immediately and enjoy your homemade Chickpea Caesar salad!" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Seared Sirloin and Creamy Shallot Sauce" _note="with Honey Lemon Roasted Carrots and Mashed Potatoes">
              <outline text="Ingredients" _note="Serves 2&#10;">
                <outline text="4 carrots" />
                <outline text="1 shallot" />
                <outline text="1/4 oz of Thyme" />
                <outline text="6 TBSP sour cream" />
                <outline text="1 unit of Beef Demi-Glace #needsattention " />
                <outline text="1 tsp of Chili Flakes" />
                <outline text="12 oz Yukon Gold Potatoes" />
                <outline text="1 lemon" />
                <outline text="2 cloves of garlic" />
                <outline text="14 oz sirloin steak" />
                <outline text="1 TBSP of honey" />
                <outline text="Also">
                  <outline text="4 tsp Vegetable Oil" />
                  <outline text="1 TBSP Butter" />
                  <outline text="Salt" />
                  <outline text="Pepper" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Adjust rack to top position and preheat oven to 425 degrees. Wash and dry all produce. Trim, peel, and slice carrots into 1-inch pieces on a diagonal. Dice potatoes into ½-inch pieces. Halve, peel, and mince shallot. Quarter lemon. Reserve 1 whole sprig thyme, then pick remaining thyme leaves from stems and roughly chop until you have 1 tsp." />
                <outline text="Toss carrots on a baking sheet with a large drizzle of oil. Season with salt and pepper. Roast until browned and tender, 25-30 minutes (after 10 minutes, we’ll start the steak)." />
                <outline text="Meanwhile, place potatoes, garlic, and reserved thyme sprig in a medium pot with enough salted water to cover by 2 inches. Boil until tender, about 15 minutes. Reserve ¼ cup cooking water, then drain. Remove and discard thyme. Return potatoes to pot and mash with 2 packets sour cream, 1 TBSP butter, salt, and pepper. Add reserved cooking water, a splash at a time, until smooth. Cover to keep warm." />
                <outline text="Once carrots have roasted 10 minutes, pat steak dry with paper towels, then season all over with salt and pepper. Heat a drizzle of oil in a large pan over medium-high heat. Add steak and cook until browned and cooked to desired doneness, 3-6 minutes per side. Transfer to a cutting board to rest at least 5 minutes. Wipe out pan." />
                <outline text="Heat a drizzle of oil in pan used for steak over medium-high heat. Add shallot and chopped thyme. Cook, stirring, until sofened, about 1 minute. Add demi-glace and ¼ cup water. Cook, stirring, until reduced, about 2 minutes. Turn of heat and stir in remaining sour cream and a squeeze of lemon juice (to taste)." />
                <outline text="Toss roasted carrots with honey, a squeeze of lemon juice, and chili flakes (to taste). Thinly slice steak against the grain. Divide carrots, mashed potatoes, and sliced steak between plates. Top steak with sauce. Serve with remaining lemon wedges on the side." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source: HelloFresh" />
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Salsa Verde Black Bean Enchiladas">
              <outline text="#vegetarian #mexican #easy " />
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="2 Scallions " />
                <outline text="1 Poblano Pepper " />
                <outline text="1 Roma Tomato " />
                <outline text="Black Beans " _note="13.4 ounce" />
                <outline text="6 Flour Tortillas " />
                <outline text="Green Salsa " _note="7.06 ounce " />
                <outline text="Monterey Jack Cheese " _note="¼ cup " />
                <outline text="Sour Cream " _note="2 tablespoon" />
                <outline text="1 Chili Pepper " />
                <outline text="Also">
                  <outline text="Tajin (spice)" />
                  <outline text="Cumin (spice)" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon on Vegetable Oil" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon of Butter" />
                  <outline text="Salt" />
                  <outline text="Pepper" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Utensils">
                <outline text="Small Bowl" />
                <outline text="Strainer" />
                <outline text="Large Pan" />
                <outline text="Potato Masher" />
                <outline text="Small pot" />
                <outline text="Baking Dish" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Step 1: Wash and Slice">
                  <outline text="Adjust rack to top position and preheat oven to 475 degrees. " _note="This might be too hot for our oven..." />
                  <outline text="Wash and dry all produce. " />
                  <outline text="Core, deseed, and dice &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;poblano&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;.">
                    <outline text="1 Poblano Pepper " />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Finely dice &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;tomato&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. ">
                    <outline text="1 Roma Tomato " />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Trim and thinly slice &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;scallions&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, separating whites from greens. ">
                    <outline text="2 Scallions " />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="&lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;Reserving liquid, &lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;drain &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;beans&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; over a small bowl.">
                    <outline text="Black Beans " _note="13.4 ounce" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Thinly slice &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;chili&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;.">
                    <outline text="1 Chili Pepper " />
                  </outline>
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 2: Soften Poblano">
                  <outline text="Heat a drizzle of oil in a large &lt;b&gt;pan&lt;/b&gt; over medium-high heat. " />
                  <outline text="Add poblano and cook until just softened, 3-4 minutes. ⏲" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 3: Add Ingredients">
                  <outline text="&lt;i&gt;Meanwhile, reserve ½ tsp Southwest &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;Spice&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; (1 tsp for 4 servings) in a second &lt;b&gt;small bowl&lt;/b&gt;. &lt;/i&gt;" />
                  <outline text="Once poblano is just softened, 👀 add to pan:">
                    <outline text="tomato" />
                    <outline text="scallion whites" />
                    <outline text="half the beans (you’ll use the rest later), " />
                    <outline text="2 TBSP reserved bean liquid, " />
                    <outline text="remaining Southwest Spice" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Cook, stirring, until fragrant and warmed through, 2-3 minutes. ⏲" />
                  <outline text="Season with salt and pepper." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 4: Make a mashed bean spread">
                  <outline text="Heat a large drizzle of oil in a small pot over medium-high heat. " />
                  <outline text="Add remaining beans and cook, stirring, until slightly softened, 2-3 minutes. ⏲" />
                  <outline text="Add 3 TBSP reserved bean liquid (⅓ cup for 4 servings; you may have some left over). " />
                  <outline text="Simmer until warmed through, 1-2 minutes. ⏲" />
                  <outline text="Reduce heat to low and stir in 1 TBSP &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;butter&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; (2 TBSP for 4). " />
                  <outline text="Turn off heat; mash with a potato masher or fork until mostly smooth. " />
                  <outline text="Season with plenty of salt and pepper." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 5: Prep Tortillas">
                  <outline text="Spread &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;tortillas&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; with mashed beans. ">
                    <outline text="6 Flour Tortillas " />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Place a small amount of &lt;b&gt;filling&lt;/b&gt; on one half of each tortilla. " />
                  <outline text="Roll up tortillas, starting with filled side, and place seam sides down in an 8-by-11-inch baking dish or ovenproof pan. ">
                    <outline text="&lt;i&gt;(For 4 servings, place in a 9-by-13-inch baking dish or two smaller ones.)&lt;/i&gt;" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Spoon enough &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;salsa&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; over to generously coat (you may have some left over). ">
                    <outline text="Green Salsa " _note="7.06 ounce " />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Sprinkle with &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;Monterey Jack&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. ">
                    <outline text="Monterey Jack Cheese " _note="¼ cup " />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Bake on top rack until salsa is bubbly and cheese melts, 3-5 minutes. ⏲" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 6: Crema">
                  <outline text="Meanwhile, add &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-red&quot;&gt;sour cream&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; to bowl with reserved Southwest Spice. ">
                    <outline text="Sour Cream " _note="2 tablespoon" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Stir in warm water 1 tsp at a time until mixture reaches a drizzling consistency. " />
                  <outline text="Season with salt and pepper. " />
                  <outline text="Top baked enchiladas with crema, scallion greens, and as much chili as you like. " />
                  <outline text="Divide between plates and serve." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/salsa-verde-enchiladas-5e838ed26d09bc180e655e61&quot;&gt;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/salsa-verde-enchiladas-5e838ed26d09bc180e655e61&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Voice Recipe 🎧">
                <outline text="This is your Podcast Guide on making Salsa Verde Black Bean Enchiladas. Let's Get Cooking!" />
                <outline text="Alright Kyle, welcome back to the kitchen! Today we're making some delicious vegetarian enchiladas with a kick - Salsa Verde Black Bean Enchiladas. Let's get started!" />
                <outline text="Step 1: Ingredients Check.">
                  <outline text="Head to the fridge and grab the following ingredients. We've got some scallions, you know, the thing that's also called green onions. a poblano pepper, the pepper that you can never find at Sprouts. And a roma tomato or two ready for chopping. The more tomatoes, the better." />
                  <outline text="Also grab the cheese. Specifically, Monterey Jack cheese from the cheese drawer for that melty goodness." />
                  <outline text="Later on, we’ll use a little sour cream for a cool crema topping, and a tablespoon of butter for the bean mash. So, might as well grab those two items as well." />
                  <outline text="Next, head over to the pantry for a can of Black beans. Or grab two cans if you’re doubling the recipe." />
                  <outline text="Don't forget the spices: ta-heen, cumin, plus some salt and pepper to taste." />
                  <outline text="Also grab the Green salsa (which is likely in the pantry, if unopened)." />
                  <outline text="That’s it from the pantry. " />
                  <outline text="In a minute, you’ll rinse and mostly drain the black beans, so make sure you have a strainer handy." />
                  <outline text="Also snatch (at least 6) flour tortillas - we'll be spreading these with a yummy black bean mash. On that same side of the kitchen, also snatch a cutting board and knife." />
                  <outline text="By now, you must have your hands full, so set all of that stuff down and we’ll finish grabbing the rest of the hardware. For instance, We'll be using a small pot (that we'll cook the beans in). A large, round, wide stove-top pan (where most of the chopped veggies will cook, not a sheet pan). You’ll also need a potato masher (or fork) that we’ll mash part of the beans with. Three small bowls (to lay of the diced veggies and crema later), the veggie chopper, and a glass baking dish. For the veggie chopper, grab the fine dice and rough dice slicers." />
                  <outline text="Once you've gathered all that stuff, we're ready to move on to the &quot;Prep&quot; phase." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="The Prep Phase!">
                  <outline text="First, let's wash and dry all our fresh produce." />
                  <outline text="Let's start with the Poblano pepper. Grab that poblano pepper and remove the core and seeds. Then, Dice it nice and small. If you don't feel like dicing, then pull out the vegetable chopper. It's literally made for this. You can dump these diced items into separate bowls." />
                  <outline text="After the pepper is diced to bits, Do the same with the roma tomato. Use a knife to slice them and the slicer to dice them…if that makes sense. I think with the tomato, use the larger dice slicer if you’re using the veggie slicer. You’re not making salsa. And dump these into a separate bowls or container." />
                  <outline text="Next? The scallions. Grab those babies and separate the white and green parts as you go. Make sure to thinly slice them both. Keep these parts separated for now. You may want to slice the scallion greens manually. They got stuck in the veggie chopper one time before. The scallion whites can chill in the veggie chopper tray while the greens sit on the chopping board. Anyways," />
                  <outline text="Before we get cooking. Grab those black beans. But Before you start draining, grab a bowl to save some of the bean liquid. Pop the can open to rinse and drain them in your strainer. You can use the lid to almost squeeze out some liquid to save and Maybe even catching some of that bean liquid as it drains. Whatever gets you the most bean juice. Then rinse the beans in the strainer and Just set both aside for now." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 1: Soften the Poblano.">
                  <outline text="Alright, let's get cooking! Grab a large pan and drizzle some oil in it. Set it to medium-high heat." />
                  <outline text="Once it's hot, toss in that diced poblano pepper. We want to cook it for about 3-4 minutes, until it's just softened." />
                  <outline text="So set a timer. This might be a good moment to clean up the trash from the veggie chopping." />
                  <outline text="To get the next ingredients ready, get the rest of the veggies on deck to be added in next. " />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 2: Flavor Explosion.">
                  <outline text="Once the poblano is softened, add in most of the diced tomato (leave some for topping later), all the scallion whites, half of the black beans, and about 2 tablespoons of the reserved bean liquid (leave up to 3 tablespoons for the mash later). Season this mixture with plenty of Ta-heen and cumin." />
                  <outline text="Give it a good stir and cook for another 2-3 minutes, until everything is fragrant and warmed through. Don't forget to season with salt and pepper!" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 3: Mashing Up the Beans.">
                  <outline text="Now for the creamy bean filling. Heat another drizzle of oil in your small pot over medium-high heat." />
                  <outline text="Add the remaining black beans and cook them, stirring occasionally, for 2-3 minutes until they're slightly softened." />
                  <outline text="Pour in 3 tablespoons of the reserved bean liquid (you might have some leftover, that's okay)." />
                  <outline text="Let it simmer for another minute or two, until everything is heated through." />
                  <outline text="Reduce the heat to low and stir in that tablespoon of butter. Mash those beans with a potato masher or fork until they're mostly smooth. Season generously with salt and pepper." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Before we start assembling, now is a good time to preheat the oven. 375° is fine." />
                <outline text="Step 4: Enchilada Time!">
                  <outline text="Time to assemble! Grab a spoon and some space to lay out your tortillas. I found it easier to also bring over both pans from the stove top over to the counter during assembly. So start by spreading a thin layer of the mashed bean mixture on each of your flour tortillas. Maybe just on the bottom half of the tortilla." />
                  <outline text="Then Spoon in the main filling mix along the top half of the tortilla, but not too much. " />
                  <outline text="Roll them up tightly, starting with the filled side, where you take the top lip to pull it down over the main filling mix, then fold in both sides and keep those sides folded as you finish rolling the burrito. Once wrapped, place them seam-side down in your baking dish." />
                  <outline text="Continue folding burritos until you run out of mix." />
                  <outline text="Once you have them all folded, grab the green salsa and pour enough green salsa over the enchiladas to coat them generously - you might have some leftover salsa, no worries!" />
                  <outline text="Next, sprinkle with that Monterey Jack cheese for that melty goodness." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 5: Baking and Crema">
                  <outline text="Pop those enchiladas on the top rack of your preheated oven and bake for 3 to 5 minutes, until the salsa is bubbly and the cheese is melted. So set another timer, here." />
                  <outline text="In the meantime, let's make that crema. Add the sour cream to a small bowl along with ta-heen and Cumin." />
                  <outline text="Stir in some warm water, one teaspoon at a time, until the crema reaches a nice drizzling consistency. Season with salt and pepper to taste." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Step 6: The Finishing Touches">
                  <outline text="Once the enchiladas are golden brown and bubbly, take them out of the oven." />
                  <outline text="Grab some plates and plate the enchiladas.  " />
                  <outline text="Top them with that delicious crema, some diced tomatoes, and some chopped scallion greens for a pop of color." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="And there you have it! Salsa Verde Black Bean Enchiladas." />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Winner Winner Chicken Orzo Dinner" _note="Chicken over Orzo Pasta with Zucchini and Tomato&#10;">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="1 Zucchini #Sprouts " />
                <outline text="Italian Seasoning #ALDI " _note="1 tbsp" />
                <outline text="1-2 Chicken Breasts #ALDI " _note="12 oz&#10;" />
                <outline text="1 lemon #Sprouts " />
                <outline text=" Panko breadcrumbs #ALDI " _note="1/4 cup" />
                <outline text="1 roma tomato #Sprouts " />
                <outline text="Orzo pasta #ALDI " _note="3 oz " />
                <outline text="Fresh Mozzarella (ball) #ALDI " _note="4 oz " />
                <outline text="Parsley #Sprouts " _note="1/4 oz " />
                <outline text="Parmesan cheese #ALDI " _note="1/4 cup" />
                <outline text="Also">
                  <outline text="4 teaspoons of Olive Oil" />
                  <outline text="Salt" />
                  <outline text="Pepper" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Utensils">
                <outline text="Baking Sheet" />
                <outline text="Large Pot" />
                <outline text="Strainer" />
                <outline text="Large Pan" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Preheat &amp; Prep">
                  <outline text="Wash and dry all produce. " />
                  <outline text="Adjust rack to upper position and preheat oven to 425 degrees. " />
                  <outline text="Bring a large pot of salted water to a boil. " />
                  <outline text="Slice zucchini into ¼-inch-thick rounds. " />
                  <outline text="Cut tomato into ½-inch-thick wedges. " />
                  <outline text="Toss zucchini and tomato with 1 TBSP olive oil and half the Italian seasoning on a baking sheet. " />
                  <outline text="Season with salt and pepper." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Roast Veggies and Cook Pasta">
                  <outline text="Roast zucchini and tomato in oven until just shy of tender, about 10 minutes. " />
                  <outline text="Once water is boiling, add half the orzo from package to pot (use the rest as you like). " />
                  <outline text="Cook, stirring occasionally, until al dente, 9-11 minutes. " />
                  <outline text="Drain, then return to pot." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Butterfly Chicken">
                  <outline text="With your hand on top of one chicken breast, cut ¾ of the way through middle, parallel to cutting board, stopping before you slice through completely. " />
                  <outline text="Repeat with other chicken breast. " />
                  <outline text="Open each up and season all over with salt, pepper, and remaining Italian seasoning." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Cook Chicken and Prep">
                  <outline text="Heat a drizzle of olive oil in a large pan over medium-high heat. " />
                  <outline text="Add chicken and cook until no longer pink in center, 3-4 minutes per side. " />
                  <outline text="Remove from pan and set aside on a plate. " />
                  <outline text="Meanwhile, cut mozzarella into ½-inch cubes. Halve lemon. Roughly chop parsley." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Broil Veggies">
                  <outline text="Once veggies have roasted 10 minutes, remove baking sheet from oven. Heat broiler to high or increase oven temperature to 500 degrees. " />
                  <outline text="Sprinkle veggies with panko, mozzarella, and Parmesan. " />
                  <outline text="Broil (or bake) until panko is golden brown, cheese is melted, and veggies are tender, 3-5 minutes." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Finish and Plate">
                  <outline text="Add juice from one lemon half and half the parsley to pot with orzo and toss to combine. " />
                  <outline text="Season to taste with salt and pepper. " />
                  <outline text="Divide orzo between plates. " />
                  <outline text="Top with veggies and chicken. " />
                  <outline text="Drizzle with any chicken juices from plate and a squeeze of lemon. " />
                  <outline text="Garnish with remaining parsley and serve." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="HelloFresh: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/winner-winner-chicken-orzo-dinner-5aaabf7530006c52b54bd0c2&quot;&gt;Webpage&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipecards/card/winner-winner-chicken-orzo-dinner-5aaabf7530006c52b54bd0c2-c62b6ab6.pdf&quot;&gt;PDF&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#kyles_favorite #chicken " />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Mark Fajitas as a cheap veggie heavy meal " />
            <outline text="Tuscan Sausage and Pepper Spaghetti" _note="with Tomatoes and Parmesan">
              <outline text="Ingredients " _note="We should only need the bell pepper. As we bought the rest of the ingredients last week. - Kyle &lt;time startYear=&quot;2022&quot; startMonth=&quot;2&quot; startDay=&quot;10&quot;&gt;Thu, Feb 10, 2022&lt;/time&gt; ">
                <outline text="1 Yellow Onion" />
                <outline text="1 Bell Pepper" />
                <outline text="Parsley" _note="¼ ounce " />
                <outline text="Diced Tomatoes" _note="¼ ounce " />
                <outline text="Tuscan Heat Spice" _note="1 tablespoon" />
                <outline text="Italian Pork Sausage" _note="9 ounce" />
                <outline text="Spaghetti" _note="6 ounce" />
                <outline text="Chicken Stock Concentrate" />
                <outline text="Parmesan Cheese" _note="¼ cup" />
                <outline text="Also">
                  <outline text="Vegetable Oil" _note="1 tablespoon" />
                  <outline text="Butter" _note="1 tablespoon" />
                  <outline text="Salt" />
                  <outline text="Pepper" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions" />
              <outline text="Source" _note="HelloFresh: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/tuscan-sausage-and-pepper-spaghetti-5d485bd59697b1000b191602&quot;&gt;Webpage&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href=&quot;https://hellofresh.com/recipecards/card/tuscan-sausage-and-pepper-spaghetti-5d485bd59697b1000b191602-8621677f.pdf&quot;&gt;PDF&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Buenos Días Black Bean Bowls " _note="with Chunky Guacamole and a Fried Egg Topper&#10;">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="2 Scallions" />
                <outline text="Black Beans" _note="13.4 oz " />
                <outline text="2 Roma Tomatoes" />
                <outline text="1 Lime" _note="Sub: Lime Juice&#10;" />
                <outline text="Jasmine Rice" _note="¾ Cup " />
                <outline text="Veggie Stock" />
                <outline text="Southwest Spice Blend" _note="Sub: 1 TBSP cumin &amp; Tajin&#10;" />
                <outline text="Sour Cream" _note="2 TBSP " />
                <outline text="Guacamole" _note="4 TBSP " />
                <outline text="2 Eggs" />
                <outline text=" Mexican Cheese Blend" _note="½ Cup" />
                <outline text="Also...">
                  <outline text="Strainer" />
                  <outline text="Medium pot" />
                  <outline text="2 Small bowls" />
                  <outline text="Large pan" />
                  <outline text="Vegetable oil (2 tsp)" />
                  <outline text="Butter (1 TBSP | 2 TBSP)" />
                  <outline text="Kosher salt" />
                  <outline text="Black pepper" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Prep">
                  <outline text="&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-gray&quot;&gt;Grab cutting board, small strainer, the 3/4 measuring cup, and two medium/small glass bowls.&lt;/mark&gt;" />
                  <outline text="Wash and dry all produce." _note="Grab black beans, scallions, roma tomato, and lime (juice).&#10;" />
                  <outline text="Trim and thinly slice 2 &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;scallions&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, separating whites from greens. " />
                  <outline text="Drain and rinse a can of black &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;beans&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Dice &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;tomato &lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;into one glass small bowl" />
                  <outline text="Quarter &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lime&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt; &lt;/mark&gt;(quarter both limes for 4 servings). " />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Cook Rice and Beans">
                  <outline text="Melt 1 TBSP &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;butter &lt;/mark&gt;🧈&lt;/b&gt; in a medium &lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-gray&quot;&gt;(rice)&lt;/mark&gt; pot over medium-high heat. " _note="(2 TBSP for 4 servings)" />
                  <outline text="Add &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;scallion&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt; &lt;/mark&gt;whites; cook, stirring occasionally, until softened, 2-3 minutes. " />
                  <outline text="Stir in ¾ Cup&amp;nbsp;of Jasmine &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;rice&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;beans&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, Veggie &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;stock&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;Tajin &amp; Cumin&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;), and ½ tsp &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;salt&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;." _note="(1¼ cups of water for 4 servings)">
                    <outline text="&lt;i&gt;Vegetable stock replaces water here. &lt;/i&gt;" />
                    <outline text="&lt;i&gt;Tajin &amp; cumin replaces Southwest Spice&lt;/i&gt;" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Bring to a boil, then cover and reduce to a low simmer. " _note="&lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-sky&quot;&gt;Stir &lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-sky&quot;&gt;often&lt;/mark&gt;, add &lt;b&gt;extra veggie stock&lt;/b&gt; as needed.&#10;" />
                  <outline text="Cook until rice is tender, 15-18 minutes. ⏲️" _note="Stir often, it sticks!" />
                  <outline text="Keep covered, off heat until ready to serve." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Make Crema">
                  <outline text="In a small bowl, combine &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;sour cream &lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;and a squeeze of &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lime juice&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Add &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;water&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt; &lt;/mark&gt;1 tsp at a time until mixture reaches a drizzling consistency" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Mix Guacamole">
                  <outline text="In a second small bowl, combine &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;guacamole&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;tomato&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, a squeeze of &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lime juice&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; to taste." />
                  <outline text="Season with &lt;b&gt;salt&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;b&gt;pepper&lt;/b&gt;." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Fry Eggs">
                  <outline text="Heat a large drizzle of &lt;b&gt;oil&lt;/b&gt; in a large pan over medium heat. ">
                    <outline text="&lt;i&gt;preferably a nonstick pan&lt;/i&gt;" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Once hot, crack &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;eggs&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt; &lt;/mark&gt;into pan, sprinkle lightly with some &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;Tajin &amp; Cumin&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;, and cover. " />
                  <outline text="Cook until whites are set but yolks are still runny, 3-4 minutes. ">
                    <outline text="&lt;i&gt;If you prefer your yolks a little more firm, carefully flip once the white is set and opaque, then let cook a couple minutes more.&lt;/i&gt;" />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Season with &lt;b&gt;salt&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Remove from heat." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Finish and Serve">
                  <outline text="Fluff&lt;b&gt; rice mixture&lt;/b&gt; with a fork; stir in half the &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;Mexican cheese&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Season with &lt;b&gt;salt&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;b&gt;pepper&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Divide between bowls; top with &lt;b&gt;guacamole&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;eggs&lt;/b&gt;, and remaining &lt;b&gt;cheese&lt;/b&gt;. " _note="(TIP: Plate everything in its own section!) &#10;" />
                  <outline text="Garnish with &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;scallion greens&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Drizzle with &lt;b&gt;crema&lt;/b&gt;. " />
                  <outline text="Serve with remaining &lt;b&gt;&lt;mark class=&quot;colored c-yellow&quot;&gt;lime wedges&lt;/mark&gt;&lt;/b&gt; on the side." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Voice Recipe 🎧">
                <outline text="Alright, let's get into the kitchen and put together these beautiful Buenos Días Black Bean Bowls. This is a fantastic, hearty dish, perfect for breakfast, lunch, or dinner, really. The combination of the savory rice and beans, the fresh, chunky guacamole, and that rich fried egg on top is just a winner." />
                <outline text="First things first, let's get our station prepped. This is what chefs call 'mise en place'—getting everything in its place before we start the actual cooking. It makes the whole process smoother. Go ahead and grab your cutting board, a small strainer, that 3/4 cup measure, and a couple of small bowls. All of that is probably in the cabinet across from the sink. And the small bowls are for the crema and guacamole, so the small/medium glass bowls are perfect, just not the really tiny bowls!" />
                <outline text="Now, let's grab of our produce. We've got scallions, a Roma tomato, and a lime. It's always a good habit to give everything a quick rinse and pat it dry." />
                <outline text="Let's pull out our other ingredients too. From the pantry find a can of black beans. Also pull out the rice, veggie stock, tajin, and cumin." />
                <outline text="Okay, let's handle the scallions. We'll trim off the root ends and any wilted tops, then thinly slice both of them. As you slice, separate the white and light green parts from the darker green tops. The reason we separate them is that the white parts are more robust, like a mild onion, and we'll cook them to build flavor. The delicate green tops are better used fresh as a garnish at the end." />
                <outline text="Next, grab that can of black beans, it should be about 13.4 oz. Pop it open and pour the beans into your strainer. Give them a good rinse under cool water. This washes away the starchy liquid from the can and removes a good deal of excess sodium, giving us a cleaner flavor to work with." />
                <outline text="Now for the Roma tomatoes. We're just going to give them both a simple dice. Try to make the pieces relatively small and even. Once they're diced, scoop them into one of your small bowls and set it aside. Finally, for this prep stage, take your lime and cut it into quarters. We'll use the juice in a few different places, so having wedges ready is perfect." />
                <outline text="With our prep done, we can start cooking. Let's get the rice and beans going. In a medium-sized pot, melt 1 tablespoon of butter over medium-high heat. Once that butter is melted and sizzling just a bit, add the scallion whites that we sliced earlier. We're going to cook these, stirring them around every now and then, for about two to three minutes. We're not looking to brown them, just to soften them up and make them fragrant. This step builds the first layer of flavor for our dish." />
                <outline text="Once the scallions are softened, it's time to add the main components. Stir in three fourths of a cup of Jasmine rice, our rinsed black beans, the veggie stock, our Southwest spice substitute—which is a tablespoon of cumin and Tajín—and about a half a teaspoon of kosher salt. The veggie stock, instead of plain water, is going to infuse the rice with a much deeper, more savory flavor. Give everything a really good stir to combine all those ingredients." />
                <outline text="Now, bring that mixture up to a boil. As soon as you see vigorous bubbles, cover the pot with a lid and immediately reduce the heat to a low simmer. It's really important that the heat is low, otherwise the bottom will scorch. This stuff likes to stick! So, for the next 15 to 18 minutes while it cooks, you'll want to give it a stir every few minutes just to make sure nothing is catching on the bottom of the pot. If it looks like it's getting a bit dry before the rice is tender, don't hesitate to add an extra splash of veggie stock or water. Once the rice is tender and the liquid is absorbed, take it off the heat but keep it covered. This lets it steam and finish cooking perfectly." />
                <outline text="While the rice is simmering, we have the perfect window to prepare our toppings. Let's make the lime crema. From the fridge, let's grab the sour cream and guacamole (or, maybe instead of guacamole, there might be avocado packets). In the other small bowl, combine 2 tablespoons of sour cream with a generous squeeze of one of your lime wedges. Whisk it together, and then add water, just 1 teaspoon at a time, until the mixture is thin enough to drizzle. You're looking for a smooth, pourable consistency, not a thick dollop. Let's give the rice a stir before we move on to the next step." />
                <outline text="Next, we'll mix our chunky guacamole. In the bowl with our diced tomatoes, add the four tablespoons of guacamole. Squeeze in a little more lime juice—this not only adds a zesty flavor but the acid also helps keep the guacamole from browning. Season it with a pinch of salt and black pepper, and gently stir it all together. You want to keep the texture nice and chunky. Speaking of nice, let's give the rice another stir." />
                <outline text="Alright, the rice is resting, the toppings are ready. It's time to fry the eggs. Heat a good drizzle of vegetable oil in a large, preferably nonstick pan over medium heat. You'll know it's ready when the oil shimmers slightly. Gently crack your two eggs into the hot pan. Be careful not to break the yolks. As they start to cook, cover the pan." />
                <outline text="We'll cook them for about three to four minutes. So give the rice another stir. Covering the pan traps steam, which helps to cook the tops of the whites so you don't have any uncooked, gelatinous bits, all while keeping the yolks beautifully runny. If you prefer your yolks a bit more firm, you can carefully flip them once the whites are set and cook for another minute or so. Before you take them out, season them with a final pinch of salt." />
                <outline text="Now for the best part: assembly. Let's go back to our pot of rice and beans. Uncover it and use a fork to fluff it up. This separates the grains. Stir in half of your half Cup of Mexican cheese blend. The residual heat from the rice will melt it, making the whole mixture creamy and delicious. Give it a taste and season with more salt and pepper if you think it needs it." />
                <outline text="To serve, divide that cheesy rice and bean mixture between two bowls. A nice tip is to arrange everything in its own section in the bowl for a really appealing presentation. So, place a generous scoop of the chunky guacamole on one side, and then carefully place a fried egg right on top of the rice. Sprinkle everything with the remaining cheese and garnish with those fresh, vibrant scallion greens we saved." />
                <outline text="For the final touch, drizzle that lime crema all over the top. Serve it immediately with the remaining lime wedges on the side, so you can add an extra squeeze of fresh citrus right before you dig in." />
                <outline text="And there you have it. A delicious, satisfying, and beautiful Buenos Días Black Bean Bowl. Enjoy." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="HelloFresh: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/buenos-dias-black-bean-bowls-5d892f9862ccf0134a593bdc&quot;&gt;Webpage&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipecards/card/buenos-dias-black-bean-bowls-5d892f9862ccf0134a593bdc-c2fa3476.pdf&quot;&gt;PDF&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#mexican #kyles_favorite #vegetarian #easy" />
              <outline text="0 Backlink" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="BBQ Rotisserie Chicken Sheet Pan Bowls">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="Chicken breast, skinless, rotisserie BBQ" />
                <outline text="Sweet Preacher Pork Rub" />
                <outline text="Worcestershire sauce " />
                <outline text="Barbecue sauce" />
                <outline text="Weis Crispy Onions" />
                <outline text="Black beans (1 can?)" />
                <outline text="Corn (Frozen Bag) " />
                <outline text="Bell peppers &amp; Onions (Pre-Mix Bag)">
                  <outline text="Available as a “pour &amp; prep” bag." />
                  <outline text="Pictsweet Farms 3 Color pepper and onion strips" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Sweet potato (pre diced)" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Instructions">
                <outline text="Spread diced sweet potatoes in a sheet pan. Add Sweet Preacher rub, and olive oil. Cook for 10 minutes.  " />
                <outline text="Shred chicken into bowl with bbq sauce, sweet preacher rub, and Worcestershire sauce for a gentle marinade." />
                <outline text="Drain and rinse black beans" />
                <outline text="After sweet potatoes are semi-soft, pour and fresh veggies (onion, bell pepper, corn) onto the sheet pan." />
                <outline text="Add some spices">
                  <outline text="Cumin" />
                  <outline text="Chili Powder" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Add chicken, and Black beans " />
                <outline text="Return sheet pan to oven." />
                <outline text="Add crispy onions (after it finishes cooking)" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="&lt;b&gt;&lt;i&gt;Alt &lt;/i&gt;&lt;/b&gt;Version">
                <outline text="Dinner BBQ Rotisserie ">
                  <outline text="Shred chicken 🍴 and put some in bowl with bbq sauce, (and Worcestershire sauce) set aside to marinade (put this in the refrigerator?)" />
                  <outline text="On a baking sheet take the &lt;b&gt;frozen sweet potatoes&lt;/b&gt; and the &lt;b&gt;onions and peppers mix&lt;/b&gt; and season with &lt;b&gt;sweet preacher seasoning&lt;/b&gt; from the pantry put in oven on 350 for about 10 mins." />
                  <outline text="Add &lt;b&gt;corn &lt;/b&gt;if I have any in the freezer" />
                  <outline text="Rinse &amp; Drain black beans." />
                  <outline text="Add a little more seasoning. (cumin and chili powder)? And sweet preacher to sheet pan." />
                  <outline text="Add chicken to pan." />
                  <outline text="Bake until sweet potatoes are soft and the beans are baked" />
                  <outline text="Go ahead and set into leftover containers" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="#kyles_favorite #easy " />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Zucchini &amp; Sun-Dried Tomato Panini " _note="with Melty Mozz, Basil Sauce &amp; Italian-Seasoned Potato Wedges">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="2 Ciabatta Rolls #ALDI" />
                <outline text="Yukon Gold Potatoes #Sprouts " _note="12 oz " />
                <outline text="1 Zucchini #Sprouts " />
                <outline text="Basil #Sprouts " _note="½ ounce " />
                <outline text="Sun-Dried Tomatoes (Jar) #Walmart " _note="1.5 ounce " />
                <outline text="Mozzarella Cheese " _note="½ cup " />
                <outline text="Mayonnaise " _note="4 tablespoon " />
                <outline text="Sour Cream " _note="2 tablespoon " />
                <outline text="Garlic Powder " _note="1 teaspoon " />
                <outline text="Italian Seasoning " _note="1 teaspoon " />
                <outline text="Also...">
                  <outline text="4 teaspoon Vegetable Oil" />
                  <outline text="1 teaspoon Olive Oil" />
                  <outline text="Salt" />
                  <outline text="Pepper" />
                  <outline text="Baking Sheet" />
                  <outline text="Large Pan" />
                  <outline text="Medium Bowl" />
                  <outline text="Small Bowl" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Roast Potatoes">
                  <outline text="Adjust rack to top position and preheat oven to 450 degrees. " />
                  <outline text="Wash and dry all produce." />
                  <outline text="Cut potatoes into ½-inch-thick wedges. " />
                  <outline text="Toss on a baking sheet with a large drizzle of oil, half the Italian Seasoning (you’ll use the rest later), salt, and pepper. " />
                  <outline text="Roast on top rack until lightly browned and tender, 20-25 minutes." />
                </outline>
                <outline text=" Prep">
                  <outline text="While potatoes roast, trim and cut zucchini into ¼-inch-thick rounds. " />
                  <outline text="Pick basil leaves from stems; finely chop leaves. " />
                  <outline text="Halve ciabatta. " />
                  <outline text="Finely chop sun-dried tomatoes." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Cook Zucchini">
                  <outline text="Toss zucchini in a medium bowl with a drizzle of olive oil, half the garlic powder (you’ll use the rest in the next step), remaining Italian Seasoning, salt, and pepper." _note="4 SERVINGS: Toss with a large drizzle of olive oil." />
                  <outline text="Heat a drizzle of oil in a large pan over medium-high heat. " />
                  <outline text="Add zucchini in a single layer; cook until golden brown and just tender, 2-4 minutes per side. " />
                  <outline text="Turn off heat; transfer to a plate. " />
                  <outline text="Wipe out pan." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Make Basil Sauce">
                  <outline text="Meanwhile, in a small bowl, combine mayonnaise, sour cream, chopped basil, and remaining garlic powder. Season with salt and pepper." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Assemble Sandwiches">
                  <outline text="Spread a layer of basil sauce onto cut sides of ciabatta." />
                  <outline text="Fill ciabatta with mozzarella, sun-dried tomatoes, and a few slices of zucchini (you may have some left over—feel free to serve on the side, if you like)." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Finish &amp; Serve">
                  <outline text="Heat a drizzle of oil in pan used for zucchini over medium heat. " />
                  <outline text="Once hot, add sandwiches; press down with a spatula or heavy-bottomed pan. Cook, pressing occasionally, until bread is toasted and cheese melts, 2-4 minutes per side. " _note="4 SERVINGS: Cook sandwiches in batches if needed." />
                  <outline text="Transfer to a cutting board. " />
                  <outline text="Once cool enough to handle, halve on a diagonal." />
                  <outline text="Divide between plates. Serve with potato wedges on the side." />
                  <outline text="&lt;i&gt;TIP: If pan seems dry when flipping the sandwiches, add another drizzle of oil or ½ TBSP butter. Lower heat if bread begins to brown too quickly.&lt;/i&gt;" />
                  <outline text="If you make a third or fourth sandwich, you can save it and have it for breakfast." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="HelloFresh: &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipes/zucchini-mozz-sun-dried-tomato-panini-5ee128214691fc476b4a9d30&quot;&gt;website&lt;/a&gt; | &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.hellofresh.com/recipecards/card/zucchini-mozz-sun-dried-tomato-panini-5ee128214691fc476b4a9d30-895495a6.pdf&quot;&gt;PDF&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Voice Recipe 🎧 ">
                <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Zucchini &amp; Sun-Dried Tomato Panini&lt;/b&gt;" />
                <outline text="Hey there, food lovers! Today, we're diving into a delicious and easy-to-make recipe: the Zucchini &amp; Sun-Dried Tomato Panini. This dish is packed with flavor and perfect for a quick and satisfying meal." />
                <outline text="Before we get started, let's gather the ingredients. From the fridge let's grab the basil, one zucchini, mayonnaise, sour cream, mozzarella cheese, and possibly the Yukon Gold potatoes. The potatoes may also be on the counter. Then over to the pantry we'll also need, a glass jar of sun-dried tomatoes, garlic powder, and Italian seasoning. Next, pull out a small baking sheet. Then, over in the bread area, snatch the the ciabatta rolls and a small bowl. Lastly, make sure you have olive oil, salt, pepper, on hand." />
                <outline text="a baking sheet, a large pan, a medium bowl, and a small bowl." />
                <outline text="Step 1." />
                <outline text="Preheat the oven to 450 degrees Fahrenheit. Wash and dry your potatoes before cutting them into ½-inch thick wedges. Toss the potatoes on a baking sheet with oil, Italian seasoning, salt, and pepper. Roast the potatoes on the top rack of the oven for 20-25 minutes, or until they're tender and lightly browned." />
                <outline text="Step 2." />
                <outline text="While the potatoes are roasting, trim and cut the zucchini into ¼-inch thick rounds. Pick the basil leaves from the stems and finely chop them. Halve the ciabatta rolls and finely chop the sun-dried tomatoes." />
                <outline text="Step 3." />
                <outline text="Toss the zucchini in a medium bowl with olive oil, garlic powder, Italian seasoning, salt, and pepper. Heat a drizzle of oil in a large pan over medium-high heat. Add the zucchini to the pan and cook until it's golden brown and tender, about 2-4 minutes per side. Remove the zucchini from the pan and set it aside on a plate." />
                <outline text="Step 4. Make the Basil Sauce." />
                <outline text="Grab a small bowl from the cabinet and a spoon from the drawer so you can spoon in about 6 spoonfuls of mayonnaise, and about half as much sour cream (so about three spoonfuls of sour cream), then slide in the chopped basil, and add some garlic powder, salt, and pepper." />
                <outline text="Step 5. Assemble the Sandwiches." />
                <outline text="Spread a layer of basil sauce on the cut sides of the ciabatta rolls. Fill the ciabatta with mozzarella cheese, sun-dried tomatoes, and zucchini slices." />
                <outline text="Step 6: Finish and Serve." />
                <outline text="Heat a drizzle of oil in the same pan you used to cook the zucchini. Add the sandwiches to the pan and press down with a spatula. Cook the sandwiches until the bread is toasted and the cheese is melted, about 2-4 minutes per side. Transfer the sandwiches to a cutting board and halve them diagonally. Serve the sandwiches with the roasted potato wedges on the side." />
                <outline text="Enjoy!" />
                <outline text="And there you have it, a delicious Zucchini &amp; Sun-Dried Tomato Panini. This recipe is easy to follow and perfect for a quick and satisfying meal. Happy cooking!" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#kyles_favorite #vegetarian #sandwich #easy" />
              <outline text="0 Backlink" />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Sides&lt;/b&gt;">
          <outline text="Kyle's Cornbread Salad">
            <outline text="Split Instructions" />
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="Cornbread" _note="3 cups, cubed (homemade or store-bought)&#10;">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://photos.google.com/photo/AF1QipMlb-iJTRk0liOVcidlYAdTguMCQAujCW3Bjomv&quot;&gt;https://photos.google.com/photo/AF1QipMlb-iJTRk0liOVcidlYAdTguMCQAujCW3Bjomv&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="Bake for 13 minutes (or until a knife comes out clean)" />
                <outline text="Kroger cornbread is around $6 per pan of 16 ounces, I bought three if I’m doubling the recipe." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Cherry tomatoes" _note="1 cup, halved&#10;" />
              <outline text="Pinto beans" _note="15 ounces, drained and rinsed&#10;" />
              <outline text="Corn" _note="15 ounces, drained&#10;" />
              <outline text="6 slices bacon, cooked and chopped" />
              <outline text="4 green onions" _note="chopped" />
              <outline text="shredded Colby Jack cheese" _note="1 ½ cups" />
              <outline text="mayonnaise" _note="1 cup " />
              <outline text="sour cream" _note="1 cup " />
              <outline text="ranch dressing powder" _note="1 package " />
              <outline text="lime juice" _note="1 tablespoon" />
              <outline text="9x9 aluminum tray">
                <outline text="Maybe a 13 x 9 cake pan?" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="The original recipe had...">
                <outline text="cheddar cheese" />
                <outline text="kidney beans" />
                <outline text="bell peppers" />
                <outline text="Inspired by &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nashphilly.com/menu&quot;&gt;https://www.nashphilly.com/menu&lt;/a&gt; ">
                  <outline text="Freshly baked cornbread crumbled, and layered with tomatoes, green onions, bell peppers, bacon, cheddar cheese, kidney beans, corn. The dressing is a mixture of sour cream, ranch, and mayo. " />
                  <outline text="" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Prepare a pan of cornbread ahead of time or use leftover cornbread you have on hand. " />
              <outline text="Cut the cooled cornbread into 1-inch pieces and place 3 cups cornbread, in a large bowl." />
              <outline text="Cook Bacon" />
              <outline text="Drain and Rinse the Pinto Beans" />
              <outline text="Slice Cherry Tomatoes in half, add into large bowl." />
              <outline text="Slice Bacon thinly and add to large bowl." />
              <outline text="Add the main ingredients to large bowl:">
                <outline text="1 cup cherry tomatoes" />
                <outline text="15 ounces pinto beans, drained and rinsed" />
                <outline text="15 ounces corn, drained" />
                <outline text="6 slices bacon" />
                <outline text="4 green onions" />
                <outline text="1 ½ cups Colby Jack cheese" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="In a second, medium bowl, combine to make the dressing:">
                <outline text="1 cup mayonnaise, " />
                <outline text="1 cup sour cream, " />
                <outline text="1 package ranch dressing powder" />
                <outline text="1 tablespoon lime juice. " />
                <outline text="Whisk together until combined." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Pour the dressing over the salad ingredients and gently toss until fully mixed." />
              <outline text="Cover and refrigerate for 2 hours or until ready to serve." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://therecipecritic.com/cornbread-salad/#wprm-recipe-container-225808&quot;&gt;https://therecipecritic.com/cornbread-salad/#wprm-recipe-container-225808&lt;/a&gt;">
              <outline text="Inspired by &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.nashphilly.com/menu&quot;&gt;https://www.nashphilly.com/menu&lt;/a&gt; " />
            </outline>
            <outline text="About">
              <outline text="I personally would like to figure out how to make some really delicious side dish that I can make in bulk that would fit in one of those industrial trays that you can scoop out to the plate very easily, and can provide to the Taylor place every month so every four weeks I would show up and provide some thing. I'm thinking about something like that corn bread casserole thing that we ate on the Square at the farmers market or something similar that I can create and save and doesn't have to be heated up." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="#easy #kyles_favorite #potluck" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Cornpone">
            <outline text="Ingredients #needsattention " />
            <outline text="Directions" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Twice-Baked New Potatoes">
            <outline text="Ingredients" _note="8 servings">
              <outline text="12 New potatoes" />
              <outline text="2 tbl of olive oil" />
              <outline text="4 oz. of cream cheese" />
              <outline text="1/2 cup of sour cream" />
              <outline text="1 1/2 cups of grated montery jack cheese" />
              <outline text="1 tbl of minced chives" />
              <outline text="1 garlic clove, minced" />
              <outline text="salt and pepper (to taste)" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Preheat oven to 375°" />
              <outline text="Drizzle potatoes with olive oil" />
              <outline text="Roast for 20 - 25 minutes" />
              <outline text="1/2 the potatoes, use a spoon to scoop the potatoes insides out. Leave the skin intact." />
              <outline text="Add cream cheese and sour cream to scooped-out potatoes." />
              <outline text="Add motery jack, chives, garlic, salt, and pepper to taste" />
              <outline text="Mash until totally smooth" />
              <outline text="Scoop into potato shells" />
              <outline text="Bake for 20 - 25 minutes" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source: Pioneer Woman" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Buttermilk Mashed Potatoes">
            <outline text="Ingredents" _note="SERVES 4">
              <outline text="2 pounds Yukon Gold potatoes, peeled and cut into 1-inch chunks" />
              <outline text="Salt and pepper" />
              <outline text="⅔ cup buttermilk, room temperature" />
              <outline text="6 tablespoons unsalted butter, melted and cooled" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="1. Place potatoes and 1 tablespoon salt in large saucepan and cover with water by 1 inch. Bring to boil over high heat, then reduce heat to medium and simmer until potatoes break apart when paring knife is inserted, about 18 minutes. Drain potatoes and return them to saucepan set on still-hot burner." />
              <outline text="2. Using potato masher, mash potatoes until few small lumps remain. Gently mix buttermilk and melted butter in small bowl until combined Add buttermilk mixture to potatoes and, using rubber spatula, gently fold until just incorporated. Season with salt and pepper to taste, and serve immediately." />
              <outline text="&lt;i&gt;To achieve the proper texture, it is important to cook the potatoes thoroughly; they are done if they break apart when a knife is inserted and gently wiggled. Buttermilk substitutes such as clabbered milk do not produce sufficiently tangy potatoes. To reduce the chance of curdling, the buttermilk must be at room temperature when mixed with the cooled melted butter.&lt;/i&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="BUTTERMILK RANCH MASHED POTATOES">
              <outline text="Add ⅓ cup sour cream, 3 very thinly sliced scallions, 2 tablespoons minced fresh parsley, and 1 minced garlic clove with buttermilk mixture in step 2." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source: Cook’s Illustrated Comfort Food Favorites 2019" />
            <outline text="" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Cheddar Biscuits with Old Bay seasoning">
            <outline text="Ingredients" _note="makes 8 biscuits">
              <outline text="Topping">
                <outline text="⅓ cup butter, melted" />
                <outline text="¼ tsp salt" />
                <outline text="¼ tsp OLD BAY® seasoning (from 6-oz container)" />
                <outline text="¼ tsp garlic powder" />
                <outline text="¼ tsp parsley flakes" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Biscuits">
                <outline text="2 cups Original Bisquick™ mix" />
                <outline text="¾ cup milk" />
                <outline text="¼ cup butter, melted" />
                <outline text="½ cup shredded Cheddar cheese (2 oz)" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions" _note=" Prep Time: 10 Min | Total 30 Min">
              <outline text="&lt;b&gt;HEAT&lt;/b&gt; oven to 425°F. In small bowl, stir together Topping ingredients; set aside." />
              <outline text="In medium bowl, stir Biscuit ingredients until soft dough forms. Drop dough by 8 spoonfuls onto ungreased cookie sheet." />
              <outline text="&lt;b&gt;BAKE&lt;/b&gt; 16 to 18 minutes or until biscuits are golden brown. Immediately brush hot biscuits with Topping. Serve warm." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source: Bisquick Box" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Flaky Buttermilk Biscuits">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="3 cups (15 ounces) King Arthur all-purpose flour" />
              <outline text="2 tablespoons sugar" />
              <outline text="4 teaspoons baking powder" />
              <outline text="½ teaspoon baking soda" />
              <outline text="1½ teaspoons salt" />
              <outline text="16 tablespoons (2 sticks) unsalted butter, frozen for 30 minutes" />
              <outline text="1¼ cups buttermilk, chilled" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="1. Line rimmed baking sheet with parchment paper and set aside. Whisk flour, sugar, baking powder, baking soda, and salt together in large bowl. Coat sticks of butter in flour mixture, then grate 7 tablespoons from each stick on large holes of box grater directly into flour mixture. Toss gently to combine. Set aside remaining 2 tablespoons butter." />
              <outline text="2. Add buttermilk to flour mixture and fold with spatula until just combined (dough will look dry). Transfer dough to well-floured counter. Dust surface of dough with flour; using your floured hands, press dough into rough 7-inch square." />
              <outline text="3. Roll dough into 12 by 9-inch rectangle with short side parallel to edge of counter. Starting at bottom of dough, fold into thirds like business letter, using bench scraper or metal spatula to release rolling into 12 by 9-inch rectangle, folding into thirds, and turning clockwise 4 more times, for total of 5 sets of folds. After last set of folds, roll dough into 8½-inch square about 1 inch thick. Transfer dough to prepared sheet, cover with plastic wrap, and refrigerate for 30 minutes. Adjust oven rack to upper-middle position and heat oven to 400 degrees." />
              <outline text="4. Transfer dough to lightly floured cutting board. Using sharp, floured chef's knife, trim ¼ inch of dough from each side of square and discard. Cut remaining dough into 9 squares, flouring knife after each cut. Arrange biscuits at least 1 inch apart on prepared sheet. Melt reserved butter; brush tops of biscuits with melted butter." />
              <outline text="5. Bake until tops are golden brown, 22 to 25 minutes, rotating sheet halfway through baking. Transfer biscuits to wire rack and let cool for 15 minutes before serving." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="" />
            <outline text="We prefer King Arthur all-purpose flour for this recipe, but other brands will work. Use sticks of butter here. In hot or humid environments, chill the flour mixture, grater, and large bowl before use. The dough will start out very crumbly and dry in pockets but will be smooth by the end of the folding process; do not be tempted to add extra buttermilk. Flour the counter and the top of the dough as needed to prevent sticking, but be careful not to incorporate large pockets of flour into the dough when folding." />
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Desserts&lt;/b&gt;">
          <outline text="Find an second birthday cake into creami recipe" />
          <outline text="Cinnamon Ice Cream">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="4 eggs" />
              <outline text="2.5 cups of sugar" />
              <outline text="4 cups heavy whipping cream" />
              <outline text="4 cups of milk" />
              <outline text="6-7 tsp vanilla extract" _note="Mother suggests less. Taste it. Stir in as needed.&#10;" />
              <outline text="1/2 tsp salt" />
              <outline text="1 Tbsp cinnamon" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Instructions">
              <outline text="Beat eggs until light. " />
              <outline text="Add sugar gradually to &quot;whole&quot; beaten eggs. " />
              <outline text="Add remaining ingredients and chill until you put it in the ice cream churn." />
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Cast-Iron Apple Nutmeg Coffee Cake ">
            <outline text="Ingredients" _note="Makes: 8 Servings">
              <outline text="Cake">
                <outline text="3 Tbsp butter, cubed" />
                <outline text="2 cups chopped peeled Gala apple" />
                <outline text="1/2 cup packed brown sugar, divided" />
                <outline text="1/4 cup brewed coffee" />
                <outline text="2/3 cup canola oil" />
                <outline text="1/2 cup sugar" />
                <outline text="1 large egg plus 1 large egg white, room temperature" />
                <outline text="2 tsp vanilla extract" />
                <outline text="1 1/2 cups all-purpose flour" />
                <outline text="2 tsp ground cinnamon" />
                <outline text="1/2 tsp salt" />
                <outline text="1/2 tsp baking soda" />
                <outline text="1/4 tsp ground nutmeg" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Drizzle">
                <outline text="1/3 cup brewed coffee" />
                <outline text="1/4 cup heavy whipping cream" />
                <outline text="1 1/2 cups confectioners' sugar" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions" _note="Prep: 25 min + Bake: 20 min + cooling">
              <outline text="Preheat the oven to 375°. In a 10-in. cast-iron or other ovenproof skillet, melt butter over low heat. Add the apples and 1/4 cup brown sugar. Cook and stir until crisp-tender, about 5 minutes. Stir in the coffee; remove from heat." />
              <outline text="In a large bowl, beat oil, sugar, egg, egg white, and vanilla and remaining 1/4 cup brown sugar until well blended. In another bowl, whisk the flour, cinnamon, salt, baking soda and nutmeg; gradually beat into oil mixture. Gently spread over apple mixture." />
              <outline text="Bake until a toothpick inserted in the center comes out clean, 18-22 minutes. Cook on a wire rack for 10 minutes." />
              <outline text="Meanwhile, for the drizzle, in a small saucepan, bring coffee and cream to a boil; cook until liquid is reduced to 1/4 cup, 10-12 minutes. Remove from heat; stir in the confectioners' sugar. Let stand for 10 minutes. Drizzle over cake." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source">
              <outline text="Taste of Home">
                <outline text="" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Strawberry Shortcake ">
            <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/easy-homemade-strawberry-shortcake/#tasty-recipes-68038&quot;&gt;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/easy-homemade-strawberry-shortcake/#tasty-recipes-68038&lt;/a&gt;" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;New / Never Made ✨&lt;/b&gt;">
            <outline text="New Orleans Beignets">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="1 pkg (1/4 oz) active dry yeast" />
                <outline text="1/4 cup warm water (110° to 115°)" />
                <outline text="1 cup evaporated milk" />
                <outline text="1/2 cup canola oil" />
                <outline text="1/4 cup sugar" />
                <outline text="1 large egg" />
                <outline text="4 1/2 cups self-rising flour" />
                <outline text="Oil for deep-fat frying" />
                <outline text="Confectioners' sugar" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="In a bowl, dissolve yeast in warm water. Add milk, oil, sugar, egg and 2 cups flour. Beat until smooth. Stir in enough remaining flour to form a soft dough (dough will be sticky). Do not knead...No knead... Cover and refrigerate overnight." />
                <outline text="Punch dough down. Turn onto a floured surface; roll into a 16x12-in rectangle. Cut into 2-in squares." />
                <outline text="In a deep cast-iron or electric skillet, heat 1 in. oil to 375°. Fry squares in batches until golden brown on both sides. Drain on paper towels. Roll the warm beignets in confectioners' sugar." />
                <outline text="Substitution Note">
                  <outline text="As a substitute for each cup of self-rising flour, place 1.5 tsp baking powder and 1/2 tsp salt in a measuring cup. Add all-purpose flour to measure 1 cup." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Taste of Home" />
                <outline text="" />
                <outline text="" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Prep: 25 min + chilling" />
              <outline text="Cook: 5 min / batch" />
              <outline text="Makes: 4 dozen" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Almost-Famous Peppermint Bark">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="12 ounces good-quality semisweet chocolate, chopped into 1/2-inch pieces" />
                <outline text="1 1/2 teaspoons peppermint extract" />
                <outline text="1 pound good-quality white chocolate, chopped into 1/2-inch pieces" />
                <outline text="3 candy canes or 12 round hard peppermint candies, crushed" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Line a 9-by-13-inch baking dish with aluminum foil, shiny-side up; smooth out any wrinkles. Heat 1 inch of water in a saucepan over low heat until steaming. Put all but 3/4 cup of the semisweet chocolate in a heatproof bowl. Set the bowl over the saucepan of steaming water (do not let the bowl touch the water) and stir until one-third of the chocolate is melted. Remove the bowl from the saucepan; keep the steaming water over low heat. Gradually stir the reserved 3/4 cup semisweet chocolate into the bowl, a few pieces at a time, until all of the chocolate is melted." />
                <outline text="Return the bowl to the saucepan, 5 to 10 seconds at a time, to help melt the chocolate, if needed. Do not rush this step: It may take up to 10 minutes to melt the chocolate. Wipe off any moisture from the bottom of the bowl. Stir 3/4 teaspoon peppermint extract into the chocolate, then quickly pour into the prepared baking dish and spread in an even layer. Firmly tap the dish against the counter to remove any air bubbles. Set aside at room temperature until almost set, about 10 minutes." />
                <outline text="Meanwhile, put all but 1 cup of the white chocolate in a large heatproof bowl and repeat the melting process over the steaming water; dry off the bottom of the bowl. Stir in the remaining 3/4 teaspoon peppermint extract; pour over the semisweet chocolate and spread in an even layer. Sprinkle immediately with the crushed candy canes, gently pressing them into the white chocolate. Set aside at room temperature until firm, about 1 hour. Lift the bark out of the pan using the foil and break it into pieces. Store in an airtight container at room temperature for up to 2 weeks." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Food Network | &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.foodnetwork.com/recipes/food-network-kitchen/almost-famous-peppermint-bark-recipe-2120609&quot;&gt;Web&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Chocolate Lava Cakes">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="Baking spray, for spraying custard cups" />
                <outline text="1 stick butter" />
                <outline text="2 ounces bittersweet chocolate" />
                <outline text="2 ounces semisweet chocolate" />
                <outline text="1 1/4 cups powdered sugar" />
                <outline text="2 whole eggs" />
                <outline text="3 egg yolks" />
                <outline text="1 teaspoon vanilla" />
                <outline text="1/2 cup all-purpose flour" />
                <outline text="Vanilla ice cream, for serving" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Preheat the oven to 425 degrees F. Spray four custard cups with baking spray and place on a baking sheet." />
                <outline text="Microwave the butter, bittersweet chocolate and semisweet chocolate in a large bowl on high until the butter is melted, about 1 minute. Whisk until the chocolate is also melted. Stir in the sugar until well blended. Whisk in the eggs and egg yolks, then add the vanilla. Stir in the flour. Divide the mixture among the custard cups." />
                <outline text="Bake until the sides are firm and the centers are soft, about 13 minutes. Let stand 1 minute. Invert on individual plates while warm and serve with vanilla ice cream." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.foodnetwork.com/recipes/chocolate-lava-cakes.html&quot;&gt;The Pioneer Woman,&lt;/a&gt; Ree Drummond : Food Network" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Servings: 4" />
              <outline text="Alternate Recipe" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/chocolate-lava-cakes/&quot;&gt;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/chocolate-&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/chocolate-lava-cakes/&quot;&gt;lava&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/chocolate-lava-cakes/&quot;&gt;-&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/chocolate-lava-cakes/&quot;&gt;cake&lt;/a&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/chocolate-lava-cakes/&quot;&gt;s/&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Maple Buttercream Icing">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="2 sticks (8 ounces) unsalted butter, at room temperature" />
                <outline text="3/4 cup powdered sugar" />
                <outline text="1 cup maple syrup" />
                <outline text="2 teaspoons vanilla extract" />
                <outline text="1/4 teaspoon kosher salt" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Combine all ingredients in the bowl of a stand mixer fitter with a whisk attachment and beat on low speed until syrup and sugar are incorporated, about 1 minute." />
                <outline text="Increase speed to high and beat until mixture is light and whipped, about 5 minutes. Use immediately to frost cupcakes, cakes, or cookies. Refrigerate any leftovers in an airtight container." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.chowhound.com/recipes/maple-buttercream-27758&quot;&gt;https://www.chowhound.com/recipes/maple-buttercream-27758&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Peach Crisp" _note="with Maple Cream Sauce">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="5 whole To 6 Whole Fresh Peaches (best When Not Overly Ripe Or Soft)" />
                <outline text="1 c. Flour" />
                <outline text="1/2 c. Sugar" />
                <outline text="1/2 c. Light Brown Sugar, Firmly Packed" />
                <outline text="1/2 tsp. Ground Cinnamon" />
                <outline text="1/2 tsp. Ground Nutmeg" />
                <outline text="1/4 tsp. Salt" />
                <outline text="1 stick Butter (1/2 Cup)" />
                <outline text="1/2 whole Lemon" />
                <outline text="7 tbsp. Real Maple Syrup, Divided" />
                <outline text="1 1/2 c. Whipping Cream" />
                <outline text="3 tbsp. Light Corn Syrup" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="In a medium bowl, mix flour, sugar, light brown sugar, cinnamon, nutmeg and salt using a fork or pastry cutter. Cut butter into small pieces and gradually add to flour mixture until evenly mixed." />
                <outline text="Peel peaches into a bowl. Add the zest from half a lemon. Squeeze juice from lemon half and stir in with peaches and zest. Add 2 tablespoons real maple syrup to peaches, stir well." />
                <outline text="Pour peach mixture into a small pan (8” or 9” square) and cover evenly with crumb topping. Cover with foil and bake at 350ºF for 15 minutes. Remove foil and bake for an additional 20 to 30 minutes or until crisp and brown on top." />
                <outline text="Maple Cream Sauce:">
                  <outline text="Pour whipping cream into a saucepan. Add 5 tablespoons real maple syrup, 3 tablespoons corn syrup and stir over moderate heat until thickened and reduced by about one-third, approximately 15 minutes. Refrigerate mixture until it is cold and thick, or set the saucepan into a small bowl of ice (the ice will melt and turn into ice water). Stirring your mixture, it will cool and thicken in about 15 minutes. Drizzle sauce over peach crisp. Serve warm." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="The Pioneer Woman" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thepioneerwoman.com/food-cooking/recipes/a10230/peach-crisp-wit/&quot;&gt;https://www.thepioneerwoman.com/food-cooking/recipes/a10230/peach-crisp-wit/&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Red Velvet Cake">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="Cake">
                  <outline text="2 1/4 cups (11 1/4 ounces) all-purpose flour" />
                  <outline text="1½ teaspoons baking soda" />
                  <outline text="Pinch salt" />
                  <outline text="1 cup buttermilk" />
                  <outline text="2 large eggs" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon distilled white vinegar" />
                  <outline text="I teaspoon vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="2 tablespoons natural unsweetened" />
                  <outline text="cocoa powder" />
                  <outline text="2 tablespoons (1 ounce) red food coloring" />
                  <outline text="12 tablespoons unsalted butter, softened " />
                  <outline text="1 1/2 cups (10 1/2 ounces) granulated sugar" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Frosting">
                  <outline text="16 tablespoons unsalted butter, softened" />
                  <outline text="4 cups (1 pound) confectioners' sugar" />
                  <outline text="1 pound cream cheese, cut into 8 pieces and softened" />
                  <outline text="1½ teaspoons vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="Pinch salt" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="1. FOR THE CAKE: Adjust oven rack to middle position and heat oven to 350 degrees. Grease two 9-inch round cake pans, line with parchment paper, grease parchment, and flour pans. Whisk flour, baking soda, and salt together in medium bowl. Whisk buttermilk, eggs, vinegar, and vanilla together in 2-cup liquid measuring cup. Mix cocoa and food coloring in small bowl until smooth paste forms." />
                <outline text="2. Using stand mixer fitted with paddle, beat butter and sugar on medium-high speed until pale and fluffy, about 3 minutes. Reduce speed to medium-low and add flour mixture in 3 additions, alternating with buttermilk mixture in 2 additions, scraping down bowl as needed. Add cocoa mixture and beat on medium speed until fully incorporated, about 30 seconds. Give batter final stir by hand. Divide batter evenly between prepared pans and smooth tops with rubber spatula. Bake until toothpick inserted in center comes out clean, about 25 minutes, rotating cakes halfway through baking. Let cakes cool in pans on wire rack for 10 minutes. Remove cakes from pans, discarding parchment, and let cool completely on rack, about 2 hours. (Cooled cakes can be wrapped tightly in plastic wrap and stored at room temperature for up to 24 hours.)" />
                <outline text="3. FOR THE FROSTING: Using stand mixer fitted with paddle, beat butter and sugar on medium-high speed until pale and fluffy, about 2 minutes. Add cream cheese, 1 piece at a time, and beat until incorporated, about 30 seconds. Beat in vanilla and salt. Refrigerate until ready to use." />
                <outline text="4. When cakes are cooled, line edges of cake platter with 4 strips of parchment to keep platter clean. Place I cake layer on platter. Spread 2 cups frosting evenly over top, right to edge of cake. Top with second cake layer, press lightly to adhere, then spread remaining frosting evenly over top and sides of cake. To smooth frosting, run edge of offset spatula around cake sides and top. Carefully remove parchment strips before serving. (Cake can be refrigerated for up to 3 days.)" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Soft and Chewy Oatmeal-Raisin Cookies">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="8 tablespoons (113g) butter, room temperature" />
                <outline text="1/4 cup (50g) granulated sugar" />
                <outline text="1/3 to 1/2 cup (71g to 106g) brown sugar, to taste" />
                <outline text="1 teaspoon &lt;a href=&quot;https://shop.kingarthurbaking.com//items/vietnamese-cinnamon&quot;&gt;cinnamon&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="1/4 teaspoon &lt;a href=&quot;https://shop.kingarthurbaking.com//items/allspice&quot;&gt;allspice&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="1/2 teaspoon &lt;a href=&quot;https://shop.kingarthurbaking.com//items/ginger&quot;&gt;ginger&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="heaping 1/2 teaspoon salt" _note="If you use salted butter, reduce salt to 1/2 level teaspoon." />
                <outline text="1/2 teaspoon baking soda" />
                <outline text="1 teaspoon &lt;a href=&quot;https://shop.kingarthurbaking.com//items/king-arthur-pure-vanilla-extract&quot;&gt;vanilla extract&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="1 large egg" />
                <outline text="3 tablespoons (64g) honey" />
                <outline text="3/4 cup (90g) &lt;a href=&quot;https://shop.kingarthurbaking.com//items/unbleached-all-purpose-flour&quot;&gt;King Arthur Unbleached All-Purpose Flour&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="1 1/2 cups (134g) quick-cooking oats" />
                <outline text="1 1/2 cups (213g) raisins (golden or regular), or currants" />
                <outline text="" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Lightly grease (or line with parchment) two baking sheets." />
                <outline text="Beat together the butter, sugars, spices, salt, baking soda, and vanilla, mixing until smooth." />
                <outline text="Beat in the egg, then the honey." />
                <outline text="Stir in the flour, then the oats, then the raisins." />
                <outline text="Cover the dough, and refrigerate it for 1 to 2 hours, until it's thoroughly chilled." _note="To save time, you can freeze unbaked cookies for 1 hour, rather than refrigerating the dough; see step 6, below." />
                <outline text="Drop the chilled dough by generous tablespoonfuls onto the prepared baking sheet; a tablespoon cookie scoop works well here. The cookies will spread, so leave 2&quot; or so between them." _note="If the dough hasn't been chilled, place the pans of shaped cookies in the freezer for 1 hour." />
                <outline text="Just before baking, preheat the oven to 375°F." />
                <outline text="Bake the cookies for 10 minutes, until they're barely beginning to brown. Reverse the pans (top to bottom, bottom to top) midway through baking. If the cookies have been frozen, bake them for 14 minutes." />
                <outline text="Remove the cookies from the oven, and cool right on the pan; or transfer to a rack if you need the pan for the next batch." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Tips from our Bakers">
                <outline text="To keep cookies soft, store them airtight at room temperature." />
                <outline text="Why the range in the amount of sugar? Some of our taste-testers thought the higher-sugar version was just too sweet, while others thought it was just right. Our advice? Try the lower-sugar version first, and see how you like it. If the cookies seem not quite sweet enough, increase the sugar next time you bake them." />
                <outline text="Looking to cut back on the sugar in your baking? See our blog post, &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.kingarthurflour.com/blog/2017/03/15/reduce-sugar-in-cookies-and-bars&quot;&gt;How to reduce sugar in cookies and bars.&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="Enjoy fresh cookies whenever you want them: Drop the balls of dough close together onto a pan. Place in the freezer. When the dough balls are frozen, wrap them airtight in a plastic bag. They'll be ready to bake (just a couple at a time in a toaster oven, if desired) whenever you get a hankering for a warm cookie." />
                <outline text="Not a fan of honey? Substitute light or dark corn syrup, or molasses." />
                <outline text="Want to substitute whole wheat flour for some (or perhaps all) of the all-purpose flour in this recipe? For best results, see &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.kingarthurflour.com/blog/2020/06/22/how-to-substitute-whole-wheat-for-white-flour-in-baking&quot;&gt;How to substitute whole wheat flour for white flour in baking&lt;/a&gt;." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="King Arthur Flour" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="The Best Creamy Chocolate Pudding">
              <outline text="Cookbook Note" _note="We prefer this pudding made with 60 percent bittersweet chocolate (our favorite products are Ghirardelli 60% Cacao Bittersweet Chocolate Premium Baking Bar and Callebaut Intense Dark Chocolate, L-60-40NV). Using a chocolate with a higher cacao percentage will result  in a thicker pudding. Our favorite Dutch-processed cocoa powder is Droste Cacao. You may substitute 1 percent or 2 percent low-fat milk for the whole milk (with a small sacrifice in richness), but do not substitute skim milk. Top with whipped cream, if desired." />
              <outline text="Ingredients" _note="SERVES 6">
                <outline text="2 teaspoons vanilla extract" />
                <outline text="½ teaspoon instant espresso powder" />
                <outline text="½ cup (3½ ounces) sugar" />
                <outline text="3 tablespoons Dutch-processed cocoa powder" />
                <outline text="2 tablespoons cornstarch" />
                <outline text="¼ teaspoon salt" />
                <outline text="½ cup heavy cream" />
                <outline text="3 large egg yolks" />
                <outline text="2½ cups whole milk" />
                <outline text="5 tablespoons unsalted butter, cut into 8 pieces" />
                <outline text="4 ounces bittersweet chocolate, chopped fine" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="1. Stir vanilla and espresso powder together in small bowl; set aside. Whisk sugar, cocoa, cornstarch, and salt together in large saucepan. Whisk in cream and egg yolks until fully incorporated, making sure to scrape corners of saucepan. Whisk in milk until incorporated." />
                <outline text="2. Place saucepan over medium heat; cook, whisking constantly, until mixture is thickened and bubbling over entire surface, 5 to 8 minutes. Cook 30 seconds longer, then remove from heat. Add butter and chocolate and whisk until melted and fully incorporated. Whisk in vanilla mixture." />
                <outline text="3. Strain pudding through fine-mesh strainer into bowl. Press lightly greased parchment paper against surface of pudding and refrigerate for at least 4 hours. Serve. (Pudding can be refrigerated for up to 2 days.)" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="The Best Soft Chocolate Chip Cookies Recipe">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="8 tablespoons of salted butter" />
                <outline text="1/2 cup white sugar (I like to use raw cane sugar with a coarser texture)" />
                <outline text="1/4 cup packed light brown sugar" />
                <outline text="1 teaspoon vanilla" />
                <outline text="1 egg" />
                <outline text="1 1/2 cups all purpose flour (more as needed – see video)" />
                <outline text="1/2 teaspoon baking soda" />
                <outline text="1/4 teaspoon salt (but I always add a little extra)" />
                <outline text="3/4 cup chocolate chips (I use a combination of chocolate chips and chocolate chunks)" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Preheat the oven to 350 degrees. Microwave the butter for about 40 seconds to just barely melt it. It shouldn’t be hot – but it should be almost entirely in liquid form." />
                <outline text="Using a stand mixer or electric beaters, beat the butter with the sugars until creamy. Add the vanilla and the egg; beat on low speed until just incorporated – 10-15 seconds or so (if you beat the egg for too long, the cookies will be stiff)." />
                <outline text="Add the flour, baking soda, and salt. Mix until crumbles form. Use your hands to press the crumbles together into a dough. It should form one large ball that is easy to handle (right at the stage between “wet” dough and “dry” dough). Add the chocolate chips and incorporate with your hands." />
                <outline text="Roll the dough into 12 large balls (or 9 for HUGELY awesome cookies) and place on a cookie sheet. Bake for 9-11 minutes until the cookies look puffy and dry and just barely golden. &lt;b&gt;Warning, friends: DO NOT OVERBAKE.&lt;/b&gt; This advice is probably written on every cookie recipe everywhere, but this is essential for keeping the cookies soft. Take them out even if they look like they’re not done yet (see picture in the post). They’ll be pale and puffy." />
                <outline text="Let them cool on the pan for a good 30 minutes or so (I mean, okay, eat four or five but then let the rest of them cool). They will sink down and turn into these dense, buttery, soft cookies that are the best in all the land. These should stay soft for many days if kept in an airtight container. I also like to freeze them." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source: Pinch of Yum">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://pinchofyum.com/the-best-soft-chocolate-chip-cookies&quot;&gt;https://pinchofyum.com/the-best-soft-chocolate-chip-cookies&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="The Ultimate Chocolate Cake">
              <outline text="Ingredients" _note="for 12 servings">
                <outline text="CAKE">
                  <outline text="1 ½ cups flour" />
                  <outline text="1 cup dutch processed cocoa powder" />
                  <outline text="1 teaspoon salt" />
                  <outline text="1 ½ teaspoons baking soda" />
                  <outline text="½ teaspoon baking powder" />
                  <outline text="1 ½ cups stout" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon espresso powder" />
                  <outline text="1 cup butter" />
                  <outline text="1 ½ cups sugar" />
                  <outline text="3 eggs" />
                  <outline text="½ cup mayonnaise" />
                  <outline text="4 oz dark chocolate, chopped" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="BUTTERCREAM">
                  <outline text="1 ½ cups butter, softened" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="½ cup dutch processed cocoa powder" />
                  <outline text="5 cups powdered sugar" />
                  <outline text="5 tablespoons milk" />
                  <outline text="fresh fruit, to decorate" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Grease and line three 8-inch (20 cm) cake tins with parchment paper." />
                <outline text="In a large bowl, sift together the flour, cocoa powder, salt, baking soda, and baking powder. Set aside." />
                <outline text="In a large glass measuring cup, combine the stout, vanilla, and espresso powder. Set aside." />
                <outline text="In a large mixing bowl, cream together the butter and sugar until it is light and fluffy, about 5 minutes, scraping down the sides occasionally to ensure all the ingredients are being evenly creamed together." />
                <outline text="Beat in the eggs, one at a time, ensuring each egg is fully incorporated before adding the next." />
                <outline text="Add the mayonnaise and beat until the mixture is smooth and creamy." />
                <outline text="Preheat oven to 325˚F (160˚C)." />
                <outline text="Alternate adding the prepared dry and wet ingredients to the batter. Starting with about ⅓ of the dry ingredients, beat until incorporated. Then add ⅓ of the liquid ingredients, mixing until the batter comes together. Continue until all the wet and dry ingredients have been incorporated." />
                <outline text="Using a rubber scraper, fold the dark chocolate chunks into the batter." />
                <outline text="Distribute the batter evenly between the 3 prepared cake tins." />
                <outline text="Bake for about 35 minutes, or until a toothpick inserted into the center of the cake comes out clean." />
                <outline text="Place the cakes on a cooling rack and leave to cool for about 10-15 minutes, until the outside of the pan is cool enough to touch." />
                <outline text="Once the cakes have cooled, loosen the edges using a butter knife. Then place a large plate over the tin and invert to release the cake from the pan. Remove the parchment paper from the bottom of the cake and slide the cake back onto the cooling rack to cool completely. Repeat for the remaining two layers." />
                <outline text="While your cakes are cooling, prepare the buttercream." />
                <outline text="In a large bowl, beat together the butter and vanilla." />
                <outline text="Gradually add in the cocoa powder, powdered sugar, and milk, continuously beating until all the ingredients have been incorporated and the frosting is light and fluffy." />
                <outline text="Frost and decorate to your liking." />
                <outline text="Slice and serve." />
                <outline text="Enjoy!" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
              <outline text="Source: Tasty">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://tasty.co/recipe/the-ultimate-chocolate-cake&quot;&gt;https://tasty.co/recipe/the-ultimate-chocolate-cake&lt;/a&gt;" _note="Includes video" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Total Time 1 hr 20 min | Prep Time 25 minutes | Cook Time 35 minutes" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Ultimate Cinnamon Buns">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="For smaller cinnamon buns, cut the dough into 12 pieces in step 3." />
                <outline text="Dough">
                  <outline text="3/4 cup warm whole milk (110 degrees)" />
                  <outline text="2¼ teaspoons instant or rapid-rise yeast" />
                  <outline text="3 large eggs, room temperature" />
                  <outline text="4¼ cups (21¼ ounces) all-purpose flour, plus extra as needed" />
                  <outline text="1/2 cup (2 ounces) cornstarch" />
                  <outline text="½ cup (3 1//2 ounces) granulated sugar" />
                  <outline text="1½ teaspoons salt" />
                  <outline text="12 tablespoons unsalted butter, cut into 12 pieces and softened" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Filling">
                  <outline text="1 1/2 cups packed (10½ ounces) light brown sugar" />
                  <outline text="1 1/2 tablespoons ground cinnamon" />
                  <outline text="1/4 teaspoon salt" />
                  <outline text="4 tablespoons unsalted butter, softened" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Glaze">
                  <outline text="1½ cups (6 ounces) confectioners' sugar" />
                  <outline text="4 ounces cream cheese, softened" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon whole milk" />
                  <outline text="1 teaspoon vanilla extract" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="1. FOR THE DOUGH: Make foil sling for 13 by 9-inch baking pan by folding 2 long sheets of aluminum foil; first sheet should be 13 inches wide and second sheet should be 9 inches wide. Lay sheets of foil in pan perpendicular to each other, with extra foil hanging over edges of pan. Push foil into corners and up sides of pan, smoothing foil flush to pan. Grease foil. Whisk milk and yeast in 2-cup liquid measuring cup until yeast dissolves, then whisk in eggs." />
                <outline text="2. Adjust oven rack to middle position and place loaf pan or cake pan on bottom of oven. Bring kettle of water to boil, Using stand mixer fitted with dough hook, mix flour, cornstarch, sugar, and salt on low speed until combined. Add milk mixture in steady stream and mix until dough comes together, about  1 minute. With mixer running, add butter, I piece at a time, and mix until incorporated. Continue to mix until dough is smooth and comes away from sides of bowl, about 10 minutes longer (if dough is still wet and sticky, add up to ¼ cup extra flour, 1 tablespoon at a time, until it clears sides of bowl). Transfer dough to counter and knead by hand to form smooth, round ball. Transfer dough ball to greased medium bowl, cover bowl tightly with plastic wrap, and transfer to middle oven rack. Pour 3 cups boiling water into loaf pan in oven, close oven door, and let dough rise until doubled in size, about 2 hours." />
                <outline text="3. FOR THE FILLING: Combine sugar, cinnamon, and salt in small bowl. Remove dough from oven and transfer to lightly floured counter. Roll dough into 18-inch square and, leaving ½-inch border around edges, spread with butter, then sprinkle evenly with sugar mixture and lightly press sugar mixture into dough. Roll dough away from you into tight cylinder, pinch lightly to seal seam, and cut into 8 pieces. Transfer pieces, cut side up, to prepared pan. Cover pan tightly with plastic and let dough rise in oven until doubled in size, about 1 hour." />
                <outline text="4. FOR THE GLAZE: Whisk all ingredients in medium bowl until smooth. Remove dough and pan with water from oven and heat oven to 350 degrees. Remove plastic and bake until buns are deep golden brown and filling is melted, 35 to 40 minutes, rotating pan halfway through baking. Transfer pan to wire rack, top buns with ½ cup glaze, and let cool for 30 minutes. Using foil overhang, lift buns out of pan. Top with remaining glaze and serve." />
                <outline text="TO MAKE AHEAD: Follow recipe through step 3, but do not allow dough to rise as directed. At this point, wrapped pan can be refrigerated for up to 24 hours. When ready to bake, let dough sit at room temperature for 1 hour, then bake as directed in step 4." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Easy Sugar Cookie Recipe (With Icing!)">
              <outline text="Ingredents">
                <outline text="Sugar Cookies">
                  <outline text="1 cup &lt;a href=&quot;https://sugarspunrun.com/salted-or-unsalted-butter-substitutions/&quot;&gt;unsalted butter&lt;/a&gt; softened to room temperature (226g)" />
                  <outline text="1 cup sugar (200g)" />
                  <outline text="1 ½ teaspoons vanilla extract²" />
                  <outline text="1 large egg" />
                  <outline text="2 ½ cups &lt;a href=&quot;https://sugarspunrun.com/how-to-measure-flour/&quot;&gt;all-purpose flour&lt;/a&gt; (315g)" />
                  <outline text="¾ teaspoon baking powder" />
                  <outline text="¾ teaspoon salt" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Sugar Cookie Frosting">
                  <outline text="3 cups powdered sugar, sifted (375g)" />
                  <outline text="3-4 Tablespoons milk" />
                  <outline text="2 Tablespoons light corn syrup" />
                  <outline text="½ teaspoon &lt;a href=&quot;https://sugarspunrun.com/make-your-own-vanilla-extract/&quot;&gt;vanilla extract&lt;/a&gt;" />
                  <outline text="Gel food coloring optional" />
                  <outline text="Additional candies and sprinkles for decorating optional" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Recommended Equipment">
                  <outline text="Kitchen Scale" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/2q88hcb&quot;&gt;(recommended but not required)&lt;/a&gt;" />
                  <outline text="Gel food coloring" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/33IGa1Q&quot;&gt;(these are the ones I use to color my sugar cookie icing)&lt;/a&gt;" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Sugar Cookies">
                  <outline text="Combine butter and sugar in the bowl of a stand mixer (or in a large bowl and use an electric hand mixer) and beat until creamy and well-combined." />
                  <outline text="Add egg and vanilla extract and beat until completely combined." />
                  <outline text="In a separate, medium-sized bowl, whisk together flour, baking powder, and salt." />
                  <outline text="Gradually add dry ingredients into wet until completely combined." />
                  <outline text="Lay out a large piece of plastic wrap and transfer approximately half of the dough onto the wrap (dough will be quite sticky at this point, that's OK!)." />
                  <outline text="Cover with clear wrap and mold into a disk.  Repeat with remaining cookie dough in another piece of cling wrap.  Transfer dough to refrigerator and chill for at least 2-3 hours3" />
                  <outline text="Once dough has finished chilling, preheat oven to 350F (175C) and line a baking sheet with parchment paper.  Set aside." />
                  <outline text="Generously dust a clean surface with flour and deposit one chilled cookie dough disk onto the surface.  Lightly flour the dough and roll out to ⅛&quot; (for thinner, crispier cookies) or ¼&quot; (for thicker, softer cookies).  Continue to add flour as needed both on top of and beneath the dough so that it doesn't stick." />
                  <outline text="Use cookie cutters to cut out shapes and use a spatula to transfer shapes to prepared baking sheet.4" />
                  <outline text="Bake on 350F (175C) for 9-10 minutes5, or until edges &lt;i&gt;just&lt;/i&gt; begin to turn lightly golden brown." />
                  <outline text="Allow cookies to cool completely on cookie sheet before moving and frosting." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Sugar Cookie Frosting">
                  <outline text="Combine sugar, 2 Tablespoons of milk, corn syrup, and vanilla extract in a medium-sized bowl and stir until combined. If frosting is too thick, add more milk, about a teaspoon at a time, until the frosting is thick but pipeable. If you accidentally add too much milk, add powdered sugar until desired texture is reached." />
                  <outline text="If coloring the frosting, divide into bowls and color as desired at this point." />
                  <outline text="Transfer frosting to a piping bag with a piping tip (I used Wilton 5), or place in a Ziploc bag and snip a small piece of the corner off (not as neat, but this will still work, just take care that the frosting isn’t so thick that it breaks open the seam of the bag when you are squeezing)." />
                  <outline text="Pipe frosting on cookies and decorate with decorative candies, if desired." />
                  <outline text="Allow frosting to harden before enjoying (this took several hours for me and may vary for you depending on the consistency of your frosting)" />
                  <outline text="Keep uneaten cookies sealed in an airtight container at room temperature." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Notes">
                  <outline text="Yield varies greatly depending both on how thick you roll the dough and the size of the cookie cutters that you use.  This recipe can be doubled." />
                  <outline text="You may also cut the vanilla extract down to ¾ teaspoon and add ¼-1/2 teaspoons of either almond or lemon extract (or just about any other extract you'd like!)" />
                  <outline text="You may chill this dough longer, overnight or even up to several days.  If the dough is too firm to roll out and starts to crack, let it rest at room temperature for about 10-15 minutes before continuing to roll." />
                  <outline text="If you don't intend to frost your cookies, then you can just sprinkle them with sanding sugar for decoration at this point." />
                  <outline text="My cookie cutters were about 3&quot; in length, if you're using smaller cookie cutters you will need to bake for less time, keep an eye on the edges of your cookies to know when they are done.I like to use Americolor gel food colorings. &lt;a href=&quot;https://amzn.to/33IGa1Q&quot;&gt;This kit&lt;/a&gt; (affiliate) includes the &quot;Red Red&quot; and &quot;Leaf Green&quot; colors that I used" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source: Sugar Spun Run">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://sugarspunrun.com/easy-sugar-cookie-recipe/&quot;&gt;https://sugarspunrun.com/easy-sugar-cookie-recipe/&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="Video" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/LNn6pUruUw0&quot;&gt;https://youtu.be/LNn6pUruUw0&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;It's Been a While 🕔&lt;/b&gt;">
            <outline text="No-Bake Cake Batter Truffles">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="½ cup unsalted butter, softend" />
                <outline text="½ cup sugar" />
                <outline text="1 tsp. vanilla" />
                <outline text="1½ cups flour" />
                <outline text="⅛ tsp. salt" />
                <outline text="1 cup yellow cake mix, dry" />
                <outline text="3 Tbsp. milk, more if needed to reach dough consistency" />
                <outline text="2 Tbsp. sprinkles" />
                <outline text="TRUFFLE COATING">
                  <outline text="16 oz white dipping chocolate" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="In a large bowl, beat together &lt;b&gt;butter&lt;/b&gt; and &lt;b&gt;sugar&lt;/b&gt; until well combined.">
                  <outline text="Beat in &lt;b&gt;vanilla&lt;/b&gt;." />
                  <outline text="Add &lt;b&gt;cake mix&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;flour&lt;/b&gt;, &lt;b&gt;salt&lt;/b&gt;, and &lt;b&gt;vanilla&lt;/b&gt; and mix thoroughly." />
                  <outline text="Add in &lt;b&gt;milk&lt;/b&gt; and mix to combine.">
                    <outline text="If needed, add more to make a dough consistency." />
                  </outline>
                  <outline text="Stir in &lt;b&gt;sprinkles&lt;/b&gt;." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Roll dough into one inch balls and place on a parchment or wax paper lined cookie sheet." />
                <outline text="Chill balls in the refrigerator or freezer for 15 minutes to firm up." />
                <outline text="While dough balls are chilling, melt almond bark in the microwave in 30 second intervals until melted.">
                  <outline text="Make sure to stir between intervals so that chocolate doesn't burn. Using a fork, or however you prefer to dip truffles, dip truffles into almond bark and shake of excess bark by tapping the bottom of the fork on the side of your bowl." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Place truffle back on the cookie sheet and top with &lt;b&gt;sprinkles&lt;/b&gt;. Repeat with remaining balls until finished." />
                <outline text="Chill and keep cake batter truffles in the refrigerator until serving in an airtight container. Store leftover truffles the same way." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="">
                <outline text="Makes around 24-30 truffles." />
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.chef-in-training.com/2011/09/no-bake-cake-batter-truffles-take-2/&quot;&gt;https://www.chef-in-training.com/2011/09/no-bake-cake-batter-truffles-take-2/&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Tres Leches Cake">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="CAKE">
                  <outline text="1 1/2 cups (6 1/4 ounces) unbleached all-purpose flour" _note="Self-rising " />
                  <outline text="2 teaspoons baking powder " _note="marked out?" />
                  <outline text="1/2 teaspoon salt" _note="marked out?" />
                  <outline text="6 large eggs, separated" />
                  <outline text="1/2 teaspoon cream of tartar, or 1/4 teaspoon lemon juice" />
                  <outline text="1 1/2 cups (10 1/2 ounces) sugar " />
                  <outline text="1/3 cup (2 5/8 ounces) cold water" />
                  <outline text="2 teaspoons vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="1 teaspoon almond flavoring" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="TRES LECHES">
                  <outline text="1 cup (8 ounces) heavy cream" />
                  <outline text="1 can (14 ounces) sweetened condensed milk" />
                  <outline text="1 small can (5 ounces) evaporated milk" />
                  <outline text="1 tablespoon brandy or light rum, or 2 teaspoons vanilla extract" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="FROSTING">
                  <outline text="4 egg whites, or 1 cup (1 1/2 ounces) meringue powder dissolved in 1/2 cup (4 ounces) cool water" />
                  <outline text="1/8 teaspoon salt" />
                  <outline text="&lt;i&gt;1 teaspoon cream of tartar (omit if using meringue powder)&lt;/i&gt;" _note="marked out?" />
                  <outline text="1 1/4 cups (8 3/4 ounces) sugar" />
                  <outline text="3 tablespoons (2 1/4 ounces) light corn syrup" />
                  <outline text="1/2 cup (4 ounces) water" />
                  <outline text="1 teaspoon vanilla extract" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Preheat the oven to 350°F." />
                <outline text="FOR THE CAKE ">
                  <outline text="In medium-sized mixing bowl, whisk together the flour, baking powder, and salt; set aside. In a large mixing bowl, beat the egg whites with the cream of tartar until soft peaks form. Set aside. In a separate bowl, beat the egg yolks until they're pale yellow and fluffy. Add the sugar and continue to beat until the mixture is very thick and falls from the beaters in ribbons." />
                  <outline text="Add the 1/3 cup cold water, vanilla, and almond to the egg yolk mixture, then stir in the dry ingredients. Gently whisk in the beaten egg whites." />
                  <outline text="Grease and flour a 9 x 13-inch pan. Spoon the batter into the pan and level it with a spatula Bake the cake for 28 to 30 minutes, until a cake tester inserted in the center comes out clean. Let the cake cool in the pan for 20 minutes." />
                  <outline text="Loosen the sides of the cake with a knife, place a serving platter upside down over the pan and gently turn the cake out onto the platter. Using a fork, poke holes all over the cake and let it cool for an additional 30 minutes." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="FOR THE TRES LECHES ">
                  <outline text="While the cake is cooling, combine the heavy cream, condensed milk, evaporated milk, and brandy in a small bowl or measuring cup with a spout. When the cake has cooled to room temperature, pour the milk mixture over it slowly, pausing occasionally to allow it to soak in. Refrigerate the cake for several hours before frosting; this allows the cake to absorb the milk." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="FOR THE FROSTING ">
                  <outline text="In a large mixing bowl, beat together the egg whites for meringue powder and water), salt, and cream of tartar until soft peaks form." />
                  <outline text="In a medium saucepan set over medium heat, bring the sugar, corn syrup, and water to a boil. Cover the pan and let the mixture boil for several minutes. Uncover the pan and boil the mixture until the temperature reaches 240°F soft-ball stage) on an instant-read or candy thermometer. Immediately remove the pan from the heat and pour the sugar syrup slowly over the egg whites, beating them on slow speed all the while. After all the sugar is mixed in, beat the frosting on high speed for several minutes, until it's thick and glossy, Stir in the vanilla." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="ASSEMBLY AND SERVING ">
                  <outline text="Spread the frosting over the cake. Serve with fresh fruit, if desired." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="The King Arthur Flour Bakers Companion All-Purpose Cookbook">
                  <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://photos.google.com/photo/AF1QipPOOXqmtj6-1Cas10vlyUZoRuSWCf7mbUa2VyTG&quot;&gt;Pic on Kyle's Google Photos&lt;/a&gt;" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="About ">
                  <outline text="This golden sponge cake, a native of Latin America, is liberally soaked with tres leches (three milks: condensed, evaporated, and heavy cream) and seems, at first read-through of the recipe, to be unbearably sweet. But the cake itself is actually only mildly sweet, allowing the sugar in the milks and frosting to contribute their share of sweetness without overwhelming the whole. For a delightful dessert, serve this with tropical fruit: mango slices, or compote of bananas and pineapple." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Chocolate Crinkles">
              <outline text="Ingredients" _note="Makes 20-30 cookies">
                <outline text="½ cup Vegetable Oil" />
                <outline text="3 squares Unsweetened Choc. (3oz.) Melted">
                  <outline text="Need to Substitute? 1 sq. Choc = 3 tbs cocoa + 1 tbs shortening" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="2 cups Sugar" />
                <outline text="4 Eggs" />
                <outline text="2 tsp Vanilla" />
                <outline text="2 cups Flour" />
                <outline text="2 tsp Baking Powder" />
                <outline text="½ tsp Salt" />
                <outline text="1 cup Powdered Sugar" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="DAY 1">
                  <outline text="Mix oil, chocolate, &amp; sugar." />
                  <outline text="Blend in 1 egg at a time until well mixed." />
                  <outline text="Add Vanilla." />
                  <outline text="Sir Flour, Baking Powder, &amp; salt into oil mixture." />
                  <outline text="Chill several hours or overnight. " />
                </outline>
                <outline text="DAY 2">
                  <outline text="Heat oven to 350 deg." />
                  <outline text="Drop tsp of dough in powdered sugar." />
                  <outline text="Roll in sugar: shape into balls" />
                  <outline text="Place about 2 inches apart on parchment paper on the pan." />
                  <outline text="Bake 10-12 min. DO NOT OVER BAKE" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Jones Family Recipe / Unknown" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#kyles_favorite " />
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Chocolate Chip Cookies">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="2 ¼ C. Self Rising Flour" />
                <outline text="1 tsp. Salt" />
                <outline text="1 tsp. Baking Soda" />
                <outline text="---" />
                <outline text="¾ C. Sugar" />
                <outline text="¾ C. Light Brown Sugar" />
                <outline text="2 Eggs" />
                <outline text="1 C. Crisco Shortening" />
                <outline text="1 tsp. Vanilla" />
                <outline text="½ tsp. Water" />
                <outline text="---" />
                <outline text="Chocolate Chips" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Preheat oven to 350 degrees" />
                <outline text="Combine first three ingredients and set aside.">
                  <outline text="2 ¼ C. Self Rising Flour " />
                  <outline text="1 tsp. Salt " />
                  <outline text="1 tsp. Baking Soda " />
                </outline>
                <outline text="In another bowl, mix the following ingredients with a mixer:">
                  <outline text="¾ C. Sugar " />
                  <outline text="¾ C. Light Brown Sugar " />
                  <outline text="2 Eggs " />
                  <outline text="1 C. Crisco Shortening " />
                  <outline text="1 tsp. Vanilla " />
                  <outline text="½ tsp. Water " />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Combine both mixtures and mix with hands, and add chips." />
                <outline text="Cook for 9 minutes" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Jones Family Recipe / Unknown" _note="Kyle always credit's Debbie Holland." />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Buttermilk Spice Cake with Cream Cheese Frosting">
              <outline text="Source: The Hungry Mouse">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.thehungrymouse.com/2009/02/10/buttermilk-spice-cake-with-cream-cheese-frosting/&quot;&gt;http://www.thehungrymouse.com/2009/02/10/buttermilk-spice-cake-with-cream-cheese-frosting/&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Let's Eat This Soon 👍&lt;/b&gt;">
            <outline text="Cake Batter Shake">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="3 tablespoons  Cool Whip Free, thawed" />
                <outline text="1⁄4 teaspoon hot powdered cocoa mix, from a 25-calorie packet (like Swiss Miss Diet or NestlÃ Fat Free)" />
                <outline text="1 tablespoon sugar-free french vanilla powdered coffee creamer" _note=" (like the kind by Coffee-mate)" />
                <outline text="1⁄2 cup light vanilla soymilk" />
                <outline text="1 tablespoon jell-o sugar-free instant vanilla pudding mix" />
                <outline text="1⁄4cup fat-free vanilla ice cream" />
                <outline text="1 1⁄2 cups crushed ice or 8 -12 ice cubes" />
                <outline text="1 tablespoon rainbow candy sprinkles (or more)" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="To make the chocolatey topping, in a small bowl, combine Cool Whip with cocoa mix. Stir until completely mixed. Cover and refrigerate." />
                <outline text="In a tall glass, combine powdered creamer with 2 tablespoons hot water and stir to dissolve. Add soymilk and pudding mix. Stir until mostly dissolved. Transfer contents of the glass to a blender." />
                <outline text="Add ice cream and ice to the blender. Blend at high speed until thoroughly mixed. If needed, remove blender from the base, stir mixture, and blend again." />
                <outline text="Pour into the tall glass. Gently stir in the sprinkles." />
                <outline text="If you want to get fancy with your chocolatey topping, spoon it into one of the bottom corners of a plastic bag; snip off the tip of that corner to create a small hole, and pipe the topping through the hole into a swirl over your shake. Or keep things simple and just spoon on the topping! If you like, top with a few extra sprinkles. Enjoy!" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Hungry Girl / Food.com" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.food.com/recipe/hungry-girls-cake-batter-shake-475553&quot;&gt;https://www.food.com/recipe/hungry-girls-cake-batter-shake-475553&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;On the Calendar 🗓&lt;/b&gt;" />
          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Recently Made 🍽&lt;/b&gt;">
            <outline text="Mixed Berry Tart">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="Pastry Cream">
                  <outline text="1 1/4 Cups whole milk, divided" />
                  <outline text="6 Tablespoons granulated sugar, divided" />
                  <outline text="Seeds scraped from 1/2 vanilla bean (or 1/2 teaspoon vanilla extract)" />
                  <outline text="5 large egg yolks" />
                  <outline text="3 Tablespoons cornstarch" />
                  <outline text="1 Package (8 ounces) cream cheese, softened and cut into cubes" />
                  <outline text="1/2 Tsp. vanilla extract" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Crust">
                  <outline text="1 Cup (2 sticks) unsalted butter, softened" />
                  <outline text="1/4 Cup granulated sugar" />
                  <outline text="1/4 Cup brown sugar" />
                  <outline text="1/4 Cup honey" />
                  <outline text="2 Cups all-pupose flour" />
                  <outline text="1/2 Cup whole wheat flour" />
                  <outline text="1/2 Tsp. salt" />
                  <outline text="1/2 Tsp. baking soda" />
                  <outline text="1/2 Tsp. cinnamon" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Topping">
                  <outline text="2 Tablespoons granulated sugar" />
                  <outline text="1 Tbsp. cornstarch" />
                  <outline text="1 Package (6 ounces) Driscoll's Blackberries" />
                  <outline text="1 Package (6 ounces) Driscoll's Blueberries" />
                  <outline text="1 Package (6 ounces) Driscoll's Raspberries" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions" _note="25 min prep + 35 min cook&#10;">
                <outline text="Pastry Cream">
                  <outline text="POUR 1 cup whole milk into a medium saucepan." />
                  <outline text="ADD 3 tablespoons granulated sugar." />
                  <outline text="ADD seeds scraped from 1/2 vanilla bean (or 1/2 teaspoon vanilla extract)." />
                  <outline text="COOK over medium heat just until mixture begins to steam. Do not overcook." />
                  <outline text="SET ASIDE milk mixture." />
                  <outline text="PLACE 5 large egg yolks into a medium bowl." />
                  <outline text="ADD 3 tablespoons cornstarch." />
                  <outline text="ADD remaining 3 tablespoons granulated sugar." />
                  <outline text="WHISK to combine." />
                  <outline text="ADD remaining 1/4 cup cold milk and WHISK until smooth." />
                  <outline text="SET ASIDE egg yolk mixture." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/4 cup milk mixture to egg yolk mixture while WHISKING continuously." />
                  <outline text="ADD another 1/4 cup milk mixture to egg yolk mixture while WHISKING continuously." />
                  <outline text="ADD all of egg yolk mixture to remaining hot milk mixture inside saucepan while WHISKING continuously." />
                  <outline text="BRING mixture to a boil while WHISKING continuously and COOK 1 – 2 minutes longer until thickened." />
                  <outline text="REMOVE from heat and ADD 1 package cream cheese and ADD 1/2 teaspoon vanilla extract." />
                  <outline text="WHISK until smooth and STRAIN through a fine mesh sieve into a bowl." />
                  <outline text="PRESS plastic wrap directly onto surface and CHILL until needed." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Crust">
                  <outline text="PLACE 1 cup (2 sticks) unsalted butter into a large bowl or the bowl of a stand mixer." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/4 cup granulated sugar." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/4 cup brown sugar." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/4 cup honey." />
                  <outline text="BEAT until smooth." />
                  <outline text="SET ASIDE butter mixture." />
                  <outline text="PLACE 2 cups all-purpose flour into a medium bowl." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/2 cup whole wheat flour." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/2 teaspoon salt." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/2 teaspoon baking soda." />
                  <outline text="ADD 1/2 teaspoon cinnamon." />
                  <outline text="WHISK to combine." />
                  <outline text="ADD half of flour mixture to butter mixture and BEAT just until incorporated. Do not over mix." />
                  <outline text="SCRAPE DOWN sides of bowl." />
                  <outline text="ADD remaining flour mixture to butter mixture and BEAT until combined." />
                  <outline text="FORM dough into a disk and WRAP in plastic wrap." />
                  <outline text="CHILL at least 1 hour until firm." />
                  <outline text="SPRINKLE work surface lightly with flour and TURN OUT dough onto work surface." />
                  <outline text="ROLL OUT dough to about 12 inches by 24 inches and about 1/8 inch thick." />
                  <outline text="CUT OUT 8 dough circles about 6 inches in diameter." />
                  <outline text="PRESS dough circles into eight 4 ½-inch tart pans with removable bottoms (or use one 10-inch tart pan)." />
                  <outline text="PRICK shells all over with a fork and CHILL30 minutes." />
                  <outline text="PREHEAT oven to 325°F." />
                  <outline text="BAKE 22 – 25 minutes or until deep golden and dry in appearance." />
                  <outline text="COOL shells inside pans on wire rack." />
                  <outline text="REMOVE shells from pans." />
                </outline>
                <outline text="Topping">
                  <outline text="PLACE 2 tablespoons granulated sugar into a small saucepan and ADD 1 tablespoon cornstarch." />
                  <outline text="WHISK thoroughly to combine." />
                  <outline text="SET ASIDE sugar mixture." />
                  <outline text="PLACE 1 package blackberries into a medium bowl and ADD 1 package blueberries and ADD 1 package raspberries." />
                  <outline text="TOSS to combine." />
                  <outline text="REMOVE 1/2 cup berry mixture and TRANSFER to saucepan containing sugar mixture and SET ASIDE remining berries." />
                  <outline text="COOK over low heat and STIR continuously until mixture comes to a boil and thickens slightly." />
                  <outline text="ALLOW berry mixture to cool completely." />
                  <outline text="POUR cooled berry mixture into remaining whole berries and STIR gently to combine." />
                  <outline text="WHISK pastry cream until smooth and SPOON about two tablespoons into each tart shell and SMOOTH surface." />
                  <outline text="SPOON about 2 tablespoons berry mixture onto each tart using a slotted spoon." />
                  <outline text="SERVE immediately." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.driscolls.com/recipes/mixed-berry-tart&quot;&gt;https://www.driscolls.com/recipes/mixed-berry-tart&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Homemade Chocolate Croissants (Pain au Chocolat) #needsattention ">
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/homemade-chocolate-croissants/&quot;&gt;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/homemade-chocolate-croissants/&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/1xPE9guwltA&quot;&gt;https://youtu.be/1xPE9guwltA&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Overnight Brioche Cinnamon Buns">
              <outline text="Ingredients" _note="9 Rolls">
                <outline text="FOR THE ROLLS">
                  <outline text="3 cups (384 g) all-purpose flour" />
                  <outline text="1.5 teaspoons kosher salt" />
                  <outline text="3 tablespoons sugar" />
                  <outline text="1.5 teaspoons instant yeast" />
                  <outline text="1 egg" />
                  <outline text="1 cup lukewarm water" />
                  <outline text="1/4 cup milk" />
                  <outline text="3 tablespoons melted butter" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="FOR THE FILLING">
                  <outline text="3/4 cups packed brown sugar" />
                  <outline text="1/8 cup cinnamon" />
                  <outline text="pinch salt" />
                  <outline text="6 tablespoons melted butter" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="FOR THE GLAZE">
                  <outline text="4 oz cream cheese, softened" />
                  <outline text="1 cups confectioners’ sugar" />
                  <outline text="1/2 teaspoon vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="pinch salt" />
                  <outline text="milk or cream to thin" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions" _note="12 hours 20 minutes">
                <outline text="In a large bowl, whisk together the flour, salt, sugar and yeast." />
                <outline text="In a separate large bowl, beat the eggs. Add the water, milk and  melted butter. Whisk to combine. Pour the liquid ingredients into the dry and mix with a rubber spatula until combined; the mixture will be wet and sticky. Cover the bowl with a bowl cover or tea towel and let rise until doubled, 2 hours." />
                <outline text="Flour a work surface generously and have more flour nearby — use as much flour as you need to prevent sticking to your hands and work surface. I can’t emphasize this enough: this is a sticky dough, and if you don’t use enough flour, it will stick. (Watch the video if you’re looking for visual guidance.)" />
                <outline text="Turn dough out onto work surface. Divide into two equal portions. At this point, you could transfer one half to a buttered loaf pan and simply bake a brioche loaf. Or, you can make two pans of cinnamon buns." />
                <outline text="Form each half into a rough ball, using flour as needed for the board and your hands. Let rest while you make the filling." />
                <outline text="Make the filling: Place the brown sugar, cinnamon and salt in a medium bowl. Pour the melted butter over top. Mix with a fork or spoon to combine. Set aside." />
                <outline text="Butter or grease with nonstick spray a 9×9- or 8×8-inch baking pan." />
                <outline text="Working with one round at a time, pat or roll out dough into a rectangle about 15×11 inches — it doesn’t have to be exact, but don’t go larger than that. Spread the filling over top, using your hands to spread if necessary. Starting at the short end, roll the rectangle into a tight coil. Cut into 9 equal pieces. Transfer to prepared pan. Repeat with remaining dough and filling. Cover each pan with plastic wrap or a bowl cover or tea towel and transfer to the fridge." />
                <outline text="The following morning, heat the oven to 375ºF." />
                <outline text="Transfer pans to the oven and bake for 25 to 30 minutes or until golden. Meanwhile, beat the cream cheese with the sugar, vanilla, and salt until light. Thin with milk if desired." />
                <outline text="Remove pan from the oven. Let buns cool briefly. Spread frosting over top or turn buns out onto a serving platter, and spread the frosting over top. Serve warm with more frosting on the side." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#PrepCook_Over_an_hour " />
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://alexandracooks.com/2018/12/24/overnight-brioche-cinnamon-rolls-with-cream-cheese-frosting/&quot;&gt;https://alexandracooks.com/2018/12/24/overnight-brioche-cinnamon-rolls-with-cream-cheese-frosting/&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="(Strawberry) Lemon Cupcakes #needsattention " _note="Needs to be updated to include strawberries &#10;">
              <outline text="Ingredients ">
                <outline text="1/2 cup (115g) unsalted butter, softened to &lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/room-temperature-butter/&quot;&gt;room temperature&lt;/a&gt;" />
                <outline text="1 cup (200g) granulated sugar" />
                <outline text="2 large eggs, at room temperature" />
                <outline text="1 and 1/2 teaspoons pure vanilla extract" />
                <outline text="1 and 1/2 cups (188g) all-purpose flour (&lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/how-to-measure-baking-ingredients/&quot;&gt;spoon &amp; leveled&lt;/a&gt;)" />
                <outline text="2 teaspoons baking powder" />
                <outline text="1/2 teaspoon salt" />
                <outline text="1/2 cup (120ml) whole milk*" />
                <outline text="1 and 1/2 Tablespoons lemon zest*" />
                <outline text="1/3 cup (80ml) fresh lemon juice*" />
                <outline text="topping: &lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/vanilla-buttercream/&quot;&gt;vanilla buttercream&lt;/a&gt; and additional lemon slices" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions ">
                <outline text="Preheat the oven to 350°F (177°C). Line a 12-cup muffin pan with cupcake liners." />
                <outline text="Using a handheld or stand mixer fitted with a paddle attachment, beat the butter and sugar together on medium-high speed in a large bowl until creamed, about 2 minutes. Scrape down the sides and bottom of the bowl as needed. Add eggs and vanilla extract, then beat on medium-high speed until combined, about 1 minute. Scrape down the sides and bottom of the bowl as needed. Set aside." />
                <outline text="Whisk the flour, baking powder, and salt together in a medium bowl. Pour the dry ingredients into the wet ingredients, start the mixer on low speed, then slowly add the milk, lemon juice, and lemon zest. Once combined, stop the mixer. Do not overmix this batter." />
                <outline text="Spoon batter evenly into 12 cupcake liners, filling them about 2/3 full. Bake for about 18-22 minutes. A toothpick inserted in the middle will come out clean when they’re done. For around 30 mini cupcakes,bake for about 11-13 minutes, same oven temperature. Remove from the oven and allow to cool completely before frosting. I usually let them cool completely in the muffin pan." />
                <outline text="Frost cooled cupcakes with &lt;a href=&quot;https://sallysbakingaddiction.com/2017/05/14/vanilla-buttercream/&quot;&gt;vanilla buttercream&lt;/a&gt;. If topping with lemon zest, do so right before serving. There may be leftover frosting depending how much you use on each cupcake." />
                <outline text="Leftover cupcakes keep well covered tightly in the refrigerator for 3 days." />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Hot Cocoa Cookies">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="For the cookies:">
                  <outline text="1/2 C unsalted sweet cream butter" />
                  <outline text="1 1/2 C nestle semi-sweet chocolate" />
                  <outline text="1 1/2 C flour" />
                  <outline text="1/4 C hershey cocoa powder" />
                  <outline text="1 1/2 tsp baking powder" />
                  <outline text="1/4 tsp salt" />
                  <outline text="1 1/4 C brown sugar" />
                  <outline text="3 large eggs" />
                  <outline text="1 1/2 tsp vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="12 large marshmallows cut in half" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="For the chocolate icing:">
                  <outline text="2 C powdered sugar" />
                  <outline text="4 tbsp unsalted sweet cream butter, melted" />
                  <outline text="1/4 C hershey cocoa powder" />
                  <outline text="1/4 C hot water" />
                  <outline text="1/2 tsp vanilla extract" />
                  <outline text="Christmas sprinkles" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="For the cookies:">
                  <outline text="In a double boiler, melt the butter and chocolate, stirring frequently. Once melted, set aside to cool" />
                  <outline text="In a medium bowl, whisk together the flour, cocoa powder, baking powder and salt." />
                  <outline text="In a standing mixer, beat the sugar, eggs and vanilla on low speed until combined." />
                  <outline text="Add the cooled chocolate and mix until combined" />
                  <outline text="Gradually add in the flour ingredients and mix until combined" />
                  <outline text="Scoop the dough (Will be brownie like texture) into a bowl and cover with foil and place into the fridge for 4 hours." />
                  <outline text="Preheat oven to 325 degrees and line a cookie sheet with parchment paper." />
                  <outline text="Using a small ice cream scoop, scoop some batter into your hands and roll into a ball" />
                  <outline text="Place cookies about 2 inches apart" />
                  <outline text="Bake the cookies for about 10 minutes" />
                  <outline text="While cookies are baking, cut the marshmallows in half" />
                  <outline text="Once cookies are baked, place a marshmallow into the center of the cookies and lightly press down" />
                  <outline text="Place cookies back into the oven for another 2-3 minutes" />
                  <outline text="Place a wire rack over another cookie sheet to allow the icing to drip off" />
                  <outline text="Once cookies are done baking let cool for a few minutes before transferring to a wire rack while you make the icing" />
                </outline>
                <outline text="For the chocolate icing:">
                  <outline text="Combine all ingredients into a large mixing bowl and mix until combined" />
                  <outline text="If the icing is still to thick, add about 3 tbsp of hot water to it to thin it out" />
                  <outline text="Using a spoon, spoon some icing onto the marshmallow on the cookie" />
                  <outline text="Repeat step number 3 onto a few more cookies" />
                  <outline text="Sprinkle a little bit of sprinkles onto the cookies" />
                  <outline text="Repeat the last two steps onto the remaining cookies. Remember to only put icing onto a few cookies at a time because the icing will dry fast." />
                  <outline text="Once all the cookies have icing and sprinkles ENJOY!" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
              <outline text="Source: Kitchen Fun With My 3 Sons">
                <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://kitchenfunwithmy3sons.com/christmas-hot-cocoa-cookies/&quot;&gt;Hot Cocoa Cookies - Melt-In-Your-Mouth Delicious - a Huge Hit for the Holidays.&lt;/a&gt;" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#holiday" />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Breads&lt;/b&gt;">
          <outline text="&lt;b&gt;New / Never Made ✨&lt;/b&gt;">
            <outline text="Cheese Bread #needsattention ">
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/aTYMZEFcOlE&quot;&gt;https://youtu.be/aTYMZEFcOlE&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Potato Cheese Bread #needsattention ">
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://youtu.be/d3sdpcaMbPk&quot;&gt;https://youtu.be/d3sdpcaMbPk&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Homemade Tortillas">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="2 cups all-purpose flour" />
                <outline text="1/2 tsp salt" />
                <outline text="3/4 cup water" />
                <outline text="3 Tbsp. olive oil" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="In a large bowl, combine flour and salt. Stir in the water and olive oil. Turn onto a floured surface; knead 10-12 times, adding a little flour or water if needed to achieve a smooth dough. Let rest for 10 minutes." />
                <outline text="Divide dough into 8 portions. On a lightly floured surface, roll each portion into a 7-in circle." />
                <outline text="In a greased cast-iron or other heavy skillet, cook tortillas over medium heat until lightly browned, 1 minute on each side. Keep warm." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Taste of Home">
                  <outline text="" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Takes: 30 Min" />
              <outline text="Makes: 8 Tortilla" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Pull-Apart Herb Bread">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="1 garlic clove, minced" />
                <outline text="1/4 cup butter, melted" />
                <outline text="2 tubes (10.2 oz each) refrigerated biscuits" />
                <outline text="1 cup shredded cheddar cheese" />
                <outline text="1/4 tsp dried basil" />
                <outline text="1/4 tsp fennel seed" />
                <outline text="1/4 tsp dried oregano" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="In a 10-in cast-iron or other ovenproof skillet, saute garlic in butter for 1 minute; remove from pan and set aside. Separate the biscuits; cut biscuits in half horizontally. Place half in an even layer in skillet; overlapping as necessary. Brush with butter mixture; sprinkle with half of the cheese and herbs. Repeat." />
                <outline text="Bake at 375° until golden brown, 20-25 minutes. Cool on wire rack; serve warm." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Taste of Home">
                  <outline text="" />
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Takes: 30 Min" />
              <outline text="Makes: 8 Servings" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Banana Bread">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="2 cups all-purpose flour" />
                <outline text="1 teaspoon baking powder" />
                <outline text="½ teaspoon baking soda" />
                <outline text="¾ cup white sugar" />
                <outline text="3 tablespoons vegetable oil" />
                <outline text="2 eggs" />
                <outline text="2 bananas, peeled and halved lengthwise" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Place ingredients in the pan of the bread machine." />
                <outline text="Select the Dough setting, and press Start. Mix the bread for 3 to 5 minutes until the bananas are mashed and all ingredients are thoroughly combined. If necessary, use a rubber spatula to push the dough from the sides of the bread pan. When 3 to 5 minutes have passed on the clock display, press Stop. Do not continue mixing. Smooth out the top of the loaf with the rubber spatula." />
                <outline text="Select the Bake setting, and press Start. The Bake cycle time may vary with machines, but should be about 50 minutes. To test the bread for doneness, insert a toothpick into the center top. Remove the toothpick. If the bread is done, the toothpick will come out clean. If there is batter on the toothpick, reset the machine on Bake and continue to bake an additional 10 to 15 minutes. Test again with the toothpick to ensure the bread is completely baked. Remove the pan from the machine, but allow the bread to remain in the pan for 10 minutes. Remove the bread to cool completely on a wire rack." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source: Allrecipes | &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.allrecipes.com/recipe/7116/banana-bread-quick-bread-for-machines/&quot;&gt;Web&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Lee's Croissants">
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="1/3 cup flour" />
                <outline text="3/4 lb butter" />
                <outline text="2 pkg yeast" />
                <outline text="1/4 Lukewarm Water" />
                <outline text="1 cup cold milk" />
                <outline text="1 tsp sugar" />
                <outline text="1 egg" />
                <outline text="2 tsp salt" />
                <outline text="3 3/4 cups flour" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="Cut butter into the flour. Place between 2 sheets waxed paper and roll into a 7 x12 inch rectangle chill while making yeast dough." />
                <outline text="dissolve yeast into warm water, and 1 cup cold milk sugar, 1 egg, salt, and remaining flour. roll into a rectangle." />
                <outline text="Peel wax off of butter. Place on the left side of the dough. Fold the right side of dough over left then Press edges together seal in butter roll to 1/3 inch thickness. Fold top 1/3 of the dough towards the center then fold bottom 1/3 over that to make 3 layers. Chill 30 minutes" />
                <outline text="Turn the dough so the open end is facing you. Roll out to 1/3 thickness and fold in 3 as before. Repeat again. chill for 30 minutes. Repeat two more times. Then roll to 1/4 thickness. Cut 20-24 squares and then cut them into 40-48 triangles. Roll loosely and shape into a crescent shape place on buttered pan let rise in a warm place (3 hrs) Bake for 15 mins at 375°" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="#sides" />
              <outline text="Servings: 40" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Really Good Garlic Bread">
              <outline text="&lt;i&gt;A 12 by 5-inch loaf of supermarket Italian bread, which has a soft, thin crust and fine crumb, works best here. Do not use a rustic or crusty artisan-style loaf. A rasp-style grater makes quick work of turning the garlic into a paste. The amount of time needed to brown the bread after flipping it in step 3 depends on the color of your baking sheet; if you use a dark-colored sheet, the browning time will be on the shorter end of the range. The test kitchen's favorite garlic powder is Spice Islands Garlic Powder.&lt;/i&gt;" />
              <outline text="Ingredients">
                <outline text="1 teaspoon garlic powder" />
                <outline text="1 teaspoon water" />
                <outline text="8 tablespoons unsalted butter" />
                <outline text="½ teaspoon salt" />
                <outline text="1/8 teaspoon cayenne pepper" />
                <outline text="4-5 garlic cloves, minced to paste (1 tablespoon)" />
                <outline text="1 (1-pound) loaf soft Italian bread, halved horizontally" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Directions">
                <outline text="1. Adjust oven rack to lower-middle position and heat oven to 450 degrees. Combine garlic powder and water in medium bowl. Add 4 tablespoons butter, salt, and cayenne to bowl; set aside." />
                <outline text="2. Place remaining 4 tablespoons butter in small bowl and microwave, covered, until melted, about 30 seconds. Stir in garlic and continue to microwave, covered, until mixture is bubbling around edges, about 1 minute longer, stirring halfway through microwaving. Transfer melted butter mixture to bowl with garlic powder-butter mixture and whisk until homogeneous loose paste forms. (If mixture melts, set aside and let solidify before using.)" />
                <outline text="3. Spread cut side of bread evenly with butter mixture. Transfer bread, cut side up, to rimmed baking sheet. Bake until butter mixture has melted and seeped into bread, 3 to 4 minutes. Remove sheet from oven. Flip bread cut side down, place second rimmed baking sheet on top, and gently press. Return sheet to oven, leaving second sheet on top of bread, and continue to bake until cut side is golden brown and crisp, 4 to 12 minutes longer, rotating sheet halfway through baking. Transfer bread to cutting board. Using serrated knife, cut each half into 8 slices. Serve immediately." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Source">
                <outline text="Cook’s Illustrated Comfort Food Favorites 2019" />
              </outline>
              <outline text="" />
              <outline text="SERVES 8" />
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Focaccia Bread">
            <outline text="3/4 cup warm water + 1/2 tsp of sugar Add 2 1/4 tsp of yeast and let bloom for 5 mins  Grease cast iron and put in 200 f oven  Don’t preheat just set the temp and run away.   " />
            <outline text="1 cup of flour and some salt Plus about 2 tablespoons of olive oil. " />
            <outline text="Add some dried rosemary if you want. Measure with your heart.  " />
            <outline text="Mix Add flour until it pulls away from the edges.  " />
            <outline text="Turn off oven and pull out pan. Hopefully it didn’t actually make it to 200  Add dough and pull and press until it covers bottom of pan and 1 inch up the side  " />
            <outline text="Let rise in oven for 20 minutes. Take out yay it was like Jesus and rose.   " />
            <outline text="Then fondle the dough until there are some divots.  " />
            <outline text="In a bowl combine some olive oil, garlic, rosemary, and Parmesan to your heart’s content.   " />
            <outline text="Oven at 400 bake for 20 mins or until the top looks like you want it." />
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Sauces / Dressings&lt;/b&gt;">
          <outline text="Garlic Peppercorn Salad Dressing">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="1 cup Buttermilk" />
              <outline text="1 cup mayo" />
              <outline text="1 tsp garlic" />
              <outline text="1 tsp black pepper" />
              <outline text="1/2 tsp salt" />
              <outline text="1/2 tsp onion powder" />
              <outline text="dash of paprika and cayenne" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Mix all ingredients together an chill." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Servings: 2" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Mimi's Coleslaw">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="2 Tab of diced onion (optional)" />
              <outline text="1/3 cup of sugar" />
              <outline text="1/4 tsp of salt" />
              <outline text="1 Tab of White Vinegar" />
              <outline text="3 T. of Oil" />
              <outline text="1/2 tsb of Poppy Seed" />
              <outline text="2/3 cup of Miracle Whip" />
              <outline text="16 oz. bag of Coleslaw" _note="&quot;I chop up the bag of coleslaw some more&quot;" />
              <outline text="Fresh Cabbage" />
              <outline text="Fresh Carrots" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source">
              <outline text="Recipe from Donna Coffman" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="#sides" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Sweet Onion Citrus Vinaigrette">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="1 clove Fresh Garlic, minced" />
              <outline text="½ Cup Sweet Onion, diced" />
              <outline text="1 whole Orange, squeezed" />
              <outline text="¼ Cup Olive Oil" />
              <outline text="¼ Cup Apple Cider Vinegar" />
              <outline text="¼ Cup Dijon Mustard" />
              <outline text="1 tbsp Honey" />
              <outline text="1 tsp Sea Salt" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Place all ingredients in a blender." />
              <outline text="Blend until smooth." />
              <outline text="Enjoy!" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source: Jess Dukes">
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.jessdukes.com/sweet-onion-citrus-viniagrette/2015/5/13/sweet-onion-citrus-vinaigrette&quot;&gt;https://www.jessdukes.com/sweet-onion-citrus-viniagrette/2015/5/13/sweet-onion-citrus-vinaigrette&lt;/a&gt;" />
              <outline text=" For lunches this week, I have put this dressing over a bed of kale and cabbage with shredded carrots, grilled chicken and almond slices. Also very good over a bed of spinach and arugula and then topped it with cherry tomatoes, reduced fat feta cheese and a quarter of an avocado!" />
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Tamarind Dipping Sauce">
            <outline text="Ingredients" _note="Makes about 2 cups">
              <outline text="5 ounces tamarind pulp" />
              <outline text="2 cups plus 2 tablespoons cold water" />
              <outline text="1 piece (1 inch) fresh ginger, peeled and grated" />
              <outline text="3 cloves garlic, minced" />
              <outline text="3 tablespoons sugar, or more to taste" />
              <outline text="1 tablespoon chili-garlic sauce (sambal oelek)" />
              <outline text="2 teaspoons Thai fish sauce (nam pla)" />
              <outline text="1 teaspoon good-quality soy sauce, such as Kikkoman" />
              <outline text="2 tablespoons cornstarch" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Step 1">
                <outline text="1. Combine the tamarind and 2 cups of the water in a small saucepan and bring to a boil over high heat. Remove the pan from the heat and let stand until the tamarind has softened, about 15 minutes. Help it along by breaking up the pulp with a spoon." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Step 2">
                <outline text="2. Strain the tamarind through a sieve, pressing on the pulp to extract as much water as possible. Discard the pump and seeds and return the water to the pan. Add the ginger, garlic, 3 tablespoons sugar, the chili-garlic sauce, fish sauce, and soy sauce and bring to a boil over high heat. Reduce the heat and simmer for 5 minutes, stirring frequently." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Step 3">
                <outline text="3. Whisk the cornstarch with the remaining 2 tablespoons water. When smooth, whisk the mixture into the tamarind sauce, continuing to whisk until the sauce thickens, about 1 minute. Remove the pan from the heat and let cool. Before serving, taste and adjust the seasoning, adding more sugar if necessary to balance the tartness of the tamarind. Store tamarind sauce in an airtight container in the refrigerator for up to a week." />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source">
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.epicurious.com/recipes/food/views/tamarind-dipping-sauce-243627&quot;&gt;https://www.epicurious.com/recipes/food/views/tamarind-dipping-sauce-243627&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Tzatziki Sauce (Greek Cucumber Salad)">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="1/2 cup plain Greek yogurt" />
              <outline text="1/2 cup cucumber, peeled, seeded, grated, and squeezed to drain" />
              <outline text="1 clove garlic, grated" />
              <outline text="1 tablespoon fresh dill, chopped" />
              <outline text="2 tablespoons lemon juice (~1/2 lemon)" />
              <outline text="salt and pepper to taste" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Mix everything and let chill in the fridge for an hour." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Options">
              <outline text="Add a splash of ouzo." />
              <outline text="Replace the dill with mint or use both dill and mint." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source: Closet Cooking">
              <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.closetcooking.com/tzatziki-sauce/?/&quot;&gt;https://www.closetcooking.com/tzatziki-sauce/?/&lt;/a&gt;" />
            </outline>
          </outline>
          <outline text="Fresh Tomato Sauce">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="Tomatoes (about 5 to 6 pounds for every quart of sauce that you want to make)">
                <outline text="You can use any type of tomato to make &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thespruceeats.com/dinner-plans-tomato-sauce-4686491&quot;&gt;tomato sauce&lt;/a&gt;, but your sauce will come together faster and easier if you use &lt;b&gt;paste&lt;/b&gt; &lt;b&gt;tomatoes&lt;/b&gt;. They have less water content and fewer seeds. But there are other &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thespruceeats.com/best-tomatoes-for-sauce-4047317&quot;&gt;types of tomatoes&lt;/a&gt; you can use." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Chopped onions (1 medium onion per 1 quart of sauce)" />
              <outline text="Minced garlic (1 to 1 1/2 cloves per quart)" />
              <outline text="Olive oil" />
              <outline text="Dried basil (1/2 teaspoon per 1 quart)" />
              <outline text="Dried &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thespruceeats.com/is-oregano-the-same-as-marjoram-1807040&quot;&gt;Oregano&lt;/a&gt; (1/2 teaspoon per 1 quart)" />
              <outline text="Salt" />
              <outline text="Pepper" />
              <outline text="You May Also Want to Add">
                <outline text="Carrots" />
                <outline text="Peppers" />
                <outline text="A pinch of sugar, if needed" />
                <outline text="Additional spices" />
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Blanch the Tomatoes">
                <outline text="Start by plunging your tomatoes into boiling water for 1 to 2 minutes. This will loosen their skins, so they're much easier to remove later. Then, rinse the tomatoes in cool water to stop the cooking process, and set them aside to cool and drain.">
                  <outline text="To safely water-bath can tomato sauce, you must add lemon juice to boost the acidity. Tomatoes aren't as acidic as they used to be. ">
                    <outline text="Refer to the &lt;a href=&quot;https://nchfp.uga.edu/how/can_03/tomato_sauce.html&quot;&gt;National Center for Home Food Preservation's guidelines&lt;/a&gt; for complete tomato canning instructions and times." />
                  </outline>
                </outline>
              </outline>
              <outline text="Remove the Skins, Stems, and Seeds">
                <outline text="Once the tomatoes are cool enough to handle, remove their skins, and cut out the spot where the stems used to attach to the tomatoes (this is referred to as coring the tomatoes). Then, slice the tomatoes in half, and scoop out the seeds. Don't worry if you miss some. A few left behind won't hurt." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Saute the Onion and Garlic">
                <outline text="Add about a 1/4 cup of &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thespruceeats.com/best-olive-oil-4690453&quot;&gt;olive oil&lt;/a&gt; to a large pot. Then, saute the onions and garlic until they're soft (a few minutes should do it)." />
                <outline text="If you'd like to add carrots or peppers to your sauce, saute them along with the onions and garlic." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Combine All the Ingredients and Cook">
                <outline text="Add the tomatoes and all of the remaining ingredients to your onion-garlic mixture, and bring the pot to a boil. Then, reduce the heat, and simmer uncovered until the sauce reaches your desired thickness. Stir frequently to keep the sauce from burning. This will take at least 2 to 3 hours but could take longer if there's a lot of water in your tomatoes (non-paste tomatoes tend to have more water content, but so do tomatoes grown during rainy years)." />
              </outline>
              <outline text="Jar Your Sauce">
                <outline text="Pour your finished sauce into jars. Use freezer jars if you want to freeze your sauce or canning jars if you'd like to can your sauce for shelf-stable storage." />
                <outline text="Want homemade tomato sauce without all the work?">
                  <outline text="Make &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thespruceeats.com/crockpot-tomato-sauce-recipe-4148262&quot;&gt;crock pot tomato sauce&lt;/a&gt;. It requires no peeling, seeding, or coring. Just stir it every so often, and you'll have a batch of sauce by the end of the day." />
                </outline>
              </outline>
            </outline>
            <outline text="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.thespruceeats.com/how-to-make-tomato-sauce-1388960&quot;&gt;https://www.thespruceeats.com/how-to-make-tomato-sauce-1388960&lt;/a&gt;" _note="Includes video" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Papa John's Pizza Sauce">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="1 (10 3/4 ounce) can tomato puree" />
              <outline text="1⁄4 cup water" />
              <outline text="1 teaspoon sugar" />
              <outline text="1 teaspoon olive oil" />
              <outline text="1⁄4 teaspoon lemon juice" />
              <outline text="1⁄4 teaspoon salt" />
              <outline text="1⁄4 teaspoon oregano" />
              <outline text="1⁄8 teaspoon basil" />
              <outline text="1⁄8 teaspoon thyme" />
              <outline text="1⁄8 teaspoon garlic powder" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Combine ingredients in a small saucepan over medium heat." />
              <outline text="Bring to a boil." />
              <outline text="Reduce heat and simmer for 15 to 20 minutes." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source: Genius Kitchen" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.food.com/recipe/papa-johns-pizza-sauce-108447&quot;&gt;https://www.food.com/recipe/papa-johns-pizza-sauce-108447&lt;/a&gt;" />
          </outline>
        </outline>
        <outline text="&lt;b&gt;Drinks&lt;/b&gt;">
          <outline text="Horchata">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="white long-grain rice" _note="1 cup, uncooked " />
              <outline text="water" _note="5 cups " />
              <outline text="milk" _note="½ cup " />
              <outline text="vanilla extract" _note="½ tablespoon " />
              <outline text="ground cinnamon" _note="½ tablespoon " />
              <outline text="white sugar" _note="⅔ cup " />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Pour the rice and water into the bowl of a blender; blend until the rice just begins to break up, about 1 minute. Let rice and water stand at room temperature for a minimum of 3 hours." />
              <outline text="Strain the rice water into a pitcher and discard the rice. Stir the milk, vanilla, cinnamon, and sugar into the rice water. Chill and stir before serving over ice." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Source" _note="&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.allrecipes.com/recipe/141828/lolas-horchata/&quot;&gt;https://www.allrecipes.com/recipe/141828/lolas-horchata/&lt;/a&gt;" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Slow Cooker Apple Cider">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="2 cinnamon sticks (3 inches)" />
              <outline text="1 teaspoon whole cloves" />
              <outline text="1 teaspoon whole allspice" />
              <outline text="2 quarts apple cider" />
              <outline text="1/2 cup packed brown sugar" />
              <outline text="1 orange, sliced" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Place cider and brown sugar in a 3-qt. slow cooker; stir until sugar dissolves. Add spice bag. Place orange slices on top. Cover and cook on low for 2-3 hours or until heated through. Discard spice bag." />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Yield: 2 quarts." />
            <outline text="Source: Taste of Home &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.tasteofhome.com/recipes/slow-cooker-cider&quot;&gt;Slow-Cooker Cider&lt;/a&gt;" />
          </outline>
          <outline text="Spiced Iced Tea">
            <outline text="Ingredients">
              <outline text="2 cups sugar" />
              <outline text="1 cup of water" />
              <outline text="1 cup lemon juice" />
              <outline text="1 1/2 cups orange juice" />
              <outline text="2 sticks cinnamon" />
              <outline text="8 cloves" />
              <outline text="1 gallon iced tea" />
              <outline text="sliced oranges" />
            </outline>
            <outline text="Directions">
              <outline text="Boil water. Add sugar to dissolve. Remove from heat and add remaining ingredients. Stir and add to tea." />
            </outline>
          </outline>
        </outline>
